window.HB_SEARCH = [{"url":"adr/adr-001-a1.html","title":"ADR-001-A1: Temporal server v1.29.7 and the workload allow-list","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-10-20, approved by the product owner. Amends ADR-001 section 3 (pin block), R7 (revisit trigger) and Annex A.2 (allow-list). Normative text: the A1 record ; explainer: A1-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"D6-K: guarded keys at v1.29.7\", \"D9: workload allow-list\", \"Rejected options\", \"Consequences and accepted residuals\". Problem The vulnerability gate flags the pinned Temporal server version (the vulnerable code path does not exist on that version, but the gate must be green). Separately, a live spike showed a relay certificate starting a Temporal system batch workflow that terminated another tenant's workflow, on every version. Decision Move the Temporal server and API pins to v1.29.7 / v1.53.0 (the SDK stays on its pin). The database schema is updated before the new binary starts. No vulnerability-gate exception: the gate must report zero findings. D6-K: the server refuses to start if any of a list of dynamic-config keys breaks its rule (for example schedules, the batcher and cross-namespace commands must be off). D9: a request that names a workflow type, a task queue or a workflow id is allowed only if every such field is on an allow-list. There is no deny-list. It covers relay starts and signals, worker polls (including the sticky-queue shape) and worker commands. The R7 revisit trigger is widened to any Go vulnerability advisory against the pinned server module. Options that lost Stay on the old version with a time-boxed exception; versions 1.29.5, 1.29.6, 1.30.8 and 1.31.4; a backport; relying on network topology; closing the hole by configuration only; a deny-list of temporal-sys-* names (everything Temporal adds is allowed until someone notices). Consequences I2 is upheld only with D9. Every new workflow type or task queue now needs a policy change with Security review and an image rebuild. Accepted risks RS1 (support horizon of 1.29 unknown), RS2 (D6-K checks only at startup), RS3 (SDK against this server proven only by one CI check), RS4 (switching off system features could break an internal workflow), RS5 (policy PR cost per new workflow type). Follow-ups F1, F2 and E4 are listed in the original and are not part of A1; A2 closes F2."},{"url":"adr/adr-001-a2.html","title":"ADR-001-A2: closing system-workflow and task-queue reach (F2)","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-10-23, approved by the product owner. Amends ADR-001-A1 (D9, D6-K) and ADR-001 Annex A.2. Normative text: the A2 record ; explainer: A2-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"D9 additions\", \"D6-K addition\", \"R-CORE\", \"Rejected options\", \"Consequences and accepted residuals\". Problem Review findings (F2-1 to F2-6) showed that a stolen service certificate could still reach Temporal system workflows, forge task tokens, read other workflows' history, or name non-platform task queues. None is a tenant-to-tenant gap; each needs a stolen certificate. Decision One record in two labelled parts. Erratum E2 covers cases where A1's wording already required the check and the D9 table left it out. Amendment A2 adds new rules. D9b+, D9d, D9e, D9f, D9g: extend the allow-list. Worker polls must not use worker versioning; every task-token API must carry a t:\u003ctenant\u003e: workflow id; history reads and core calls must name a t:\u003ctenant\u003e: workflow id; shutdown and heartbeat calls may name only platform or sticky-shaped queues. D6-K addition: the server refuses to start unless token-namespace enforcement is on. R-CORE: the core builds every workflow id it sends to Temporal from the tenant resolved from the validated token and a key read under row-level security, and never forwards a client-supplied opaque token. This is a code-and-test rule, not an Authorizer rule. A drift test lists every field of every allow-listed request, so a Temporal API upgrade that adds a field fails CI until a policy change classifies it. Options that lost Closing F2-1 with a config key; both the rule and the key; comparing namespace ids in the Authorizer; deny-listing system names; a per-tenant certificate for the core; leaving F2-2 and F2-4 as residuals. Consequences I2 is strengthened for the core-to-Temporal path. No new start or signal path (I6 unchanged). Accepted risks # Risk Revisit when RS6 R-CORE is enforced by code and tests, because the Authorizer cannot see inside a history page token. The first core call to Temporal, any paginating core API, or any path running tenant code. RS7 Under D9g, one worker pod can still unload a sibling pod's SDK-shaped sticky queue, because the check covers the shape, not the owner. Harmless: sticky dispatch falls back to the normal queue. Worker certificates become per-pod or per-tenant, or sticky queues gain a tenant meaning. RS2 (widened) The new D6-K row is checked only at startup, like the rest. Any runtime write path to dynamic config."},{"url":"adr/adr-001-a5.html","title":"ADR-001-A5: a cascade cycle suppresses the re-start","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED (revision 1), 2026-10-31, accepted by the Technical Lead under sign-off the product owner delegated for this amendment only. Amends ADR-001 I9 and Annex B CI-10. Normative text: the A5 record ; explainer: A5-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"Context\", \"Consequences\", \"Rejected options\", \"Accepted residuals\", \"Recorded dissent\". Problem I9 said the pipeline rejects a cycle (the same origin seen again on the same record). That rejects a normal workflow: a user creates a record, a definition starts, and its own \"update this record\" step writes the record again under the same origin. Decision A workflow definition starts at most once per (tenant, origin, record). The check runs when the pipeline is about to start a workflow, not when it writes. On a repeat the start is suppressed, the write still commits, and one audit row is recorded. Every write made by a workflow is one level deeper than the delivery that caused it, so signals between running workflows also hit the depth limit, which is unchanged. Mechanics (A5-1 to A5-7): the check lives in the event sink inside the triggering write's transaction; a new tenant table cascade_start holds the key; a depth-0 write always mints an origin id; a compiler-enforced claim token is required to enqueue a start; causation is carried through starts and signals. Options that lost Keep the old I9 text; check on writes with a trigger id (the Principal Software Engineer's proposal); reject the write on a hit; a unique index on the outbox; an advisory lock; stamping every workflow write with the start's causation. Consequences Starts are idempotent per (tenant, origin, record, definition). Cascades terminate. One small row is kept per workflow start, indefinitely in the first milestone. Accepted risks RS-A5-1: a definition that legitimately needs to run twice on one record in one origin cannot. RS-A5-2: signal ping-pong between open runs is bounded by the depth limit, not the start key. RS-A5-3: a stolen worker certificate could forge a lower depth, which is already inside ADR-001 R8. Recorded dissent: the Principal Software Engineer proposed carrying the trigger on writes; the CTO declined it as unnecessary."},{"url":"adr/adr-001-a6.html","title":"ADR-001-A6: audit roles and start rows not tied to a record","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-02, by the Solution Architect with ADR-004 (no human sign-off under the current policy). Amends ADR-001 Annex A.2 (producer rules) and A.3 (role table). Companion: ADR-004 (audit log). Normative text: the A6 record ; explainer: A6-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"A6.1\", \"A6.2\", \"A6.3\", \"Invariant check\", \"Acceptance tests\". Problem The audit log design needs three things ADR-001's role and outbox rules did not allow: a role to erase audit diffs, tenant onboarding that can seed the audit sealer, and an outbox start row for a workflow (the sealer) that is not about a record. Decision A6.1: a new database role nava_erasure , a one-shot job role. It holds no membership, ownership or BYPASSRLS ; it can read audit columns only (never the diff or its salt), perform the erase transition, and record its own erase entry. Its credential lives with the job runner, never on an app or worker host. A6.2: nava_onboard gets INSERT on outbox (only rows of kind audit.seal , for a tenant that is still provisioning, enforced by the policy) and on audit_chain_head . No SELECT on outbox . Seeding is idempotent through the head's primary key. A6.3: a start row not tied to a record takes target_seq from its own head row's version under that row's lock, and inserts no workflow_ref . Everything else in A.2 still applies. Not part of this amendment: allowing audit.seal in the Temporal workload allow-list, which is a separate policy change under A1 . Consequences D22, the workflow-id scheme, the relay and the 10-entry policy allow-list are unchanged. I1, I2, I3, I6 and I7 hold. Accepted risks Whoever holds the erasure credential can erase and record a valid erase entry; that shows up as an erasure but is still loss of evidence."},{"url":"adr/adr-001-e1.html","title":"ADR-001-E1: command inspection for continue-as-new","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED (inherited from ADR-001), dated 2026-10-15. This is an inline erratum, not a separate file: it is the section E1 (2026-10-15) at the end of ADR-001. Parent: ADR-001 . The CTO ruled on it and the product owner approved it with the first plan; Security supports it. Paraphrases: ADR-001 \"Errata\", \"E1 (2026-10-15): command inspection for continue-as-new\". What it changes ADR-001 said the Temporal Authorizer should deny a continue-as-new command \"that changes the workflow id\". That clause cannot fire, because a continue-as-new command has no workflow-id field: Temporal always keeps the id. Decision The clause is replaced by rules that are strictly stronger. For a worker's RespondWorkflowTaskCompleted , the Authorizer: denies the response, and alerts, if the task token does not decode or its workflow id has no t:\u003ctenant\u003e: prefix; denies start-child, signal-external and cancel-external commands whose target has another tenant's prefix (or none) or another namespace; denies continue-as-new whose nava-tenant header is missing or differs from the task token's prefix, or that sets a cron schedule; denies every command type not on the written allow-list. Consequences The header check is defence in depth, because the worker sets the header; the real controls are the namespace check and deny-unlisted. Annex B row CI-6 is updated to match. I6, I7 and D22 are unchanged. Accepted risks None recorded in this erratum."},{"url":"adr/adr-001-e3.html","title":"ADR-001-E3: the onboarding role reads its own tenant row","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-10-25, approved by the product owner. Erratum to ADR-001 Annex A.3, onboarding row. Normative text: the E3 record ; explainer: E3-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"Context\", \"Consequences\", \"Rejected options\", \"Accepted residual\". Problem Annex A.3 said the onboarding role may SELECT \"its own provisioning row\". The shipped policy has no state test, and a state test cannot be added: activating a tenant is an UPDATE , and PostgreSQL applies the SELECT policy to the new row too, so a provisioning-only policy rejects the switch to active (error 42501). The idempotency check also needs to read a tenant that may already be active. Decision The words change; the code does not. The clause now reads: SELECT on its own row (the tenant of its transaction, in either state). No migration, grant or policy changes. Options that lost Adding state = 'provisioning' to the policy (breaks activation); the same with an unfiltered UPDATE (works only until a statement names a column); a second permissive policy for active rows (identical to the shipped policy, with more surface). Consequences Text now matches code. A test pins that the role sees only its own tenant, in both states, and nothing with no tenant set. Accepted risks RS8: the onboarding role bound to tenant A can read A's active row (id, state, request hash, created time), nothing else. Severity Info. It holds only while the caller cannot choose the tenant id, which the server derives itself."},{"url":"adr/adr-001.html","title":"ADR-001: Platform blueprint","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-10-15, approved by the product owner. Changing an invariant or a decision now needs a superseding ADR signed by the product owner. Normative text: ADR-001-platform-blueprint.md . Plain-language explainer: ADR-001-explained.html (source ADR-001-explained.md ). Supporting material, not summarised here: rfc/ and spikes/ . Each section below names the ADR heading it paraphrases. Where wording matters, read the original. Problem Paraphrases: \"1. Invariants\", \"TICK-2 gap coverage\". A multi-tenant ticketing platform must keep one tenant's data away from every other tenant on every path: the database, the identity provider, the workflow engine and the API. ADR-001 fixes the rules every later design has to keep, and four decisions that make them real. The invariants Paraphrases: \"1. Invariants\". Annex A.2 and A.3 were changed later by A1, A2, E3 and A6, so a row here is not always the current rule on its own; read the amendments below. # Rule, in short I1 The tenant wall is in the database: every tenant table has a tenant_id and forced row-level security. With no tenant context a query gets no rows or an error. I2 The tenant comes only from a platform-verified source (a validated token resolved through tenant_identity_binding , or a row the platform wrote). Never from client values, payloads or workflow inputs. I3 Database roles have least privilege: no superuser, no BYPASSRLS , no ownership, no role membership, no grants to PUBLIC . Every grant comes from one manifest. No SECURITY DEFINER function or RLS-bypassing view is reachable from a runtime role. I4 One write pipeline with optimistic concurrency; a stale write is a typed conflict. I5 One authorization layer, inside the query. Unreadable, other-tenant and missing records give the same response. I6 The durable (workflow) path is deterministic and versioned, and is entered only through the transactional outbox. I7 Every side effect is idempotent. I8 Contracts are the boundary; configuration, not code; AI is advisory. I9 Cascades are bounded by a causation record and a platform depth limit (amended by A5 ). Options and decision Paraphrases: \"2. Decisions\", \"4. Rejected options\". Decision What it says Main alternatives that lost D26 tenant resolution One shared Keycloak realm per installation (version 26.1.3 or later), one organization per tenant. The tenant is resolved from the token issuer and organization id through tenant_identity_binding ; the token must name exactly one organization, otherwise 401. Fails closed. Realm per tenant; tenant id as a user attribute; auto-linking brokered logins by email; Zitadel instead of Keycloak. D6 API REST-first /api/v1 from an OpenAPI 3.1 contract, additive-only within a major version, RFC 9457 errors, opaque cursors, Idempotency-Key , ETag/ If-Match , per-tenant rate limits. GraphQL-first, or REST and GraphQL equal in the first milestone. D22 Temporal access One Temporal namespace per installation, mutual TLS with a deny-by-default Authorizer that also inspects worker commands. Only the relay starts and signals workflows, from outbox rows. Workflow ids are t:\u003ctenant\u003e:\u003ctarget_key\u003e . Namespace per tenant; tenant in workflow input; network isolation alone; an Authorizer that checks only the API. D28 database roles One database role per system actor. No role has BYPASSRLS , owns anything, or is a member of any role. Backups are physical, through a REPLICATION -only role. A BYPASSRLS system role; logical-dump backups; SECURITY DEFINER functions. The identity provider is reached only through two core interfaces, IdentityResolver and DirectoryAdmin (Annex A.4), so a later switch is a contained adapter change. Consequences Paraphrases: \"3. Consequences\". The project owns a custom Temporal server build (Authorizer and ClaimMapper) as a separate Go module, re-verified on every Temporal upgrade. Every cross-tenant database path can be listed, and CI fails on any new one. Keycloak is administered only through the platform API in the first milestone. Per-target FIFO signalling costs a little on outbox inserts; the worst case must be bounded. Accepted risks Paraphrases: \"5. Residuals\". Each was accepted by the product owner on 2026-10-15. # Risk Revisit when R1 Temporal histories are cross-tenant data and are not encrypted in the first milestone. The first regulated-data tenant. R2 The migrator is all-powerful over its tables; the backup holds every byte. Any request to run DDL from a runtime process. R3 No tenant self-administration of Keycloak. Epic 26 scoping. R7 A custom Temporal server build to own. Every Temporal upgrade. R8 A stolen relay, core or worker certificate has wide reach (see the original for each). Any new holder of a Temporal certificate. R9 Head-of-line blocking per target while a signal backs off. The Epic 08 flood test. No dissent was recorded. Amendments and errata Listed in date order. ADR-001's signed text is not edited; each record sits beside it. Record Date What it changes ADR-001-E1 2026-10-15 Replaces a continue-as-new check that could not fire with stronger Authorizer rules (inline erratum). ADR-001-A1 2026-10-20 New Temporal server pins, a startup guard, and a workload allow-list. ADR-001-A2 2026-10-23 Closes system-workflow and task-queue reach through service certificates. ADR-001-E3 2026-10-25 Wording fix: the onboarding role reads its own tenant row in either state. ADR-001-A5 2026-10-31 A cascade cycle suppresses the re-start instead of rejecting the write. ADR-001-A6 2026-11-02 New erasure role; onboarding seeds the audit sealer; start rows not tied to a record. A3 and A4 were skipped to avoid confusion with Annex A.3 and A.4 (see the A5 record). Erratum E2 has no file of its own: it is part of the A2 record."},{"url":"adr/adr-002-e1.html","title":"ADR-002-E1: a line-scoped nolint replaces the api.go exclusion","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-10-28, approved by the product owner, who accepted residual R-e. Erratum to ADR-002 : replaces its \"Lint rule\" design row and closes its residual R-b. Normative text: the E1 record ; explainer: E1-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"Context\", \"Consequences\", \"How a second GroupPaths caller is caught\", \"Rejected options\", \"Accepted residual\". Problem ADR-002 let one handler call the group-path reader by excluding the whole of internal/server/api.go from a lint rule. That also switched off the file's other two rules. Security raised it as a Low finding, not exploitable today. Decision Remove the per-file exclusion. The one call in GetMe carries a line-scoped //nolint:forbidigo directive, word for word as in the record. Enable the nolintlint linter with specific, explained, not-unused directives. A CI script, run with the lint step, fails closed on three checks: the set of such directives must be exactly that one line (in any spelling); the lint config may have no new exclusion; and the only generated Go files may be the two known ones (a fake generated-file header would silence every linter). ADR-002's acceptance-criterion row for the lint rules is reworded to include the gate. R-b is closed and replaced by R-e. Options that lost Keeping the file exclusion; narrowing it by text; moving GetMe to its own excluded file; a directive plus a count-only gate (bypassable by moving the directive or changing its case); a function-scoped custom analyzer (the right shape, deferred until a second waiver is needed). Consequences No runtime code changes. I5 is strengthened. Adding a second waiver means editing the gate, which is a reviewed CI change. Accepted risks R-e: the gate pins the line text, not its location, so the line could be moved to another function in the file. Review of that file is the control. Severity Low. Revisit when a second waiver is needed or this happens once; then adopt the custom analyzer."},{"url":"adr/adr-002.html","title":"ADR-002: /me shows the caller's group paths","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-10-27, approved by the product owner, who accepted residuals R-a to R-d. Normative text: ADR-002-me-group-paths.md ; explainer: ADR-002-explained.html . Supporting material: rfc/ , spikes/ . Errata: ADR-002-E1 (2026-10-28). Each section names the ADR heading it paraphrases. Problem Paraphrases: \"1. Decision\", \"8. Options considered and rejected\". The story-5 acceptance step \"Alice's /me shows group service-desk\" could not be shown: a person could not read their own group memberships without running SQL. Decision Paraphrases: \"1. Decision\", \"2. Contract\", \"3. Design\". GET /api/v1/me gains group_paths , an always-present array of {id, path} . The existing groups array is unchanged. It lists only the caller's own groups, in the caller's own tenant, checked in SQL under row-level security. The Keycloak adapter's /t/\u003ctenant_id\u003e prefix is stripped, so the adapter's layout is not part of the additive-only v1 contract. Paths are for display only. No authorization decision, ACL, cache key or metric may key on a path. This is a new contract field and read port, so it is its own ADR rather than an amendment of ADR-001 . No invariant changes. How \"display only\" is enforced (section 5): a test pins the fields of the authorization principal; type-qualified lint rules allow one caller of the path reader; a later ACL-epic acceptance criterion requires the ACL compiler to use group ids only. Options that lost Paraphrases: \"8. Options considered and rejected\". Amending the story instead; a group_paths string array parallel to groups (aligned only by position); last-segment names (ambiguous); keeping the tenant prefix; putting paths in the principal; a path reader fed from a caller-supplied id list (would let a member list any group); a 500 when the prefix is missing; a GET /groups/{id} endpoint. Consequences Paraphrases: \"4. Acceptance criteria\", \"6. CTO sign-off on protected paths\", \"10. Invariant check\". One short extra transaction on /me only. Isolation tests gain a tenant-B marker group and an exact-membership-set check. A path without the expected prefix is dropped and logged (never logging the path). Accepted risks Paraphrases: \"7. What is being accepted\". # Risk Why accepted R-a A tenant admin can name a subgroup so its stripped path looks like another tenant's. It affects only that tenant's own members. R-b Any handler in one file may call the path reader. Lint exclusions are per file. Closed by ADR-002-E1 . R-c The \"starts with /\" and \"never null\" rules are not enforced at runtime. Unit tests cover the cases that matter. R-d Membership is read in a second transaction, so group_paths can lag groups by one request. Display data may lag."},{"url":"adr/adr-003.html","title":"ADR-003: retiring recorded interpreter histories","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-01, approved by the product owner, who accepted residuals RS-1 to RS-4. A standalone ADR, not an ADR-001 amendment. Normative text: ADR-003-retiring-recorded-histories.md ; explainer: ADR-003-explained.html . Supporting material: rfc/ , spikes/ . It has no amendments or errata. Each section names the ADR heading it paraphrases. Problem Paraphrases: \"2. Context\". A replay gate replays every committed workflow history against the current interpreter code, and forbids editing or deleting any history already on main . The older rule for retiring an old code arm was to delete the GetVersion branch; after that the old histories no longer replay, but cannot be deleted either. The pipeline document marked this as open. A spike also showed that deleting the call is unsafe: old histories replay green, and a production run the query missed would silently run the new arm. Decision Paraphrases: \"1. Decision\", \"3. Design\". Retire a GetVersion change's old arm only by raising minSupported ; the call itself is never deleted. In the same PR, delete each history that now fails replay with the \"removed support of version -1\" error, and append one tombstone per file to an append-only manifest. A test enforces this mechanically (rules R-1 to R-8: no edits to histories on the base branch, no deletion without a matching tombstone, the manifest only grows, retired names do not reappear, the call remains with a raised minimum, arguments are literals, and the deleted blob really fails with that error). Approval is human and outside CI: the Technical Architect reviews, the Operator posts the production query evidence, and only the human owner merges a PR touching the manifest. Scope: only the default-version arm. Options that lost Paraphrases: \"5. Options considered and rejected\". Deleting the call; moving retired files to a subfolder; an allow-list in Go code; re-recording histories; enforcing approval through code owners (agent PRs share the owner's GitHub account); running the production query from CI; a 24-hour freshness window for the evidence. Consequences Paraphrases: \"4. Consequences\", \"8. Rollout and sequencing\", \"9. Invariant check\". Test code, test data and docs only, with no production code change. It matters at the first GetVersion retirement. A missed run blocks on replay instead of running the wrong code. Accepted risks Paraphrases: \"7. What is being accepted\". # Risk Revisit when RS-1 \"Agents never merge a manifest PR\" is enforced by process only, not by GitHub. Agents get their own GitHub identity. RS-2 The production query is a human-checked fact; a missed run blocks after deploy and recovery is a fix forward. The first retirement. RS-3 Only the default-version arm is in scope. The first need to retire a later version. RS-4 Whether a reset run's start time falls before the cut-off is not verified, so any reset invalidates the evidence. Only if resets become routine."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-a1.html","title":"ADR-004-A1: only nava_audit_reader reads the raw diff","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-04, by the Solution Architect after all three reviewers approved. Amendment to ADR-004 : amends R7, R12, R14, CI-A3 and the audit_log_select and entity_definition_versions_select policies. Normative text: the A1 record ; explainer: A1-explained.html . Corrected on one point by ADR-004-E8 (the reader's column list). Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\", \"Options considered\", \"Accepted risks\". Problem ADR-004 masks secret pointers in audit_log.diff when it is read, and only internal/audit/read runs the masker. But nava_app also held column SELECT on diff and diff_salt , so any nava_app query elsewhere returned the raw diff. Row-level security limits rows, not columns, and the static guard reads Go source, so it cannot see SQL built at runtime. Nothing read the diff outside the reader, so this closed a latent risk, not a live exploit. Decision A dedicated login role, nava_audit_reader , is the only runtime role that can read audit_log.diff and diff_salt . It has its own PgBouncer login and its own connection pool, held by internal/audit/read . nava_app loses both column grants. The role has SELECT only, on listed audit_log columns and on entity_definition_versions (definition_id, schema) ; no memberships, no other grants. audit_log_select is limited to nava_worker , nava_erasure and nava_audit_reader ; nava_app gets row access only through the generated writer policy. The masker fails closed: if a definition has no visible version row, the request fails with a 500 and returns no diff. The pool is capped at 5 connections, in PgBouncer and in the Go pool. The reader's DSN is read only by the config loader. Record history is two transactions: nava_app checks the record first (same 404 for missing, unreadable and other-tenant), then the reader pool reads entries. CI-A3 gains a catalogue check that no other runtime role holds SELECT on diff or diff_salt , on the table or any partition, and that the policy's role list is exact. The earlier static guard stays as a second layer. The role, the revoke and the reader pool land in one PR, because between them the audit endpoints would return 500. Options that lost A narrow SECURITY DEFINER function (breaks the no-definer-function rule); SET ROLE (needs a role membership, also forbidden); masking in a database view or function; keeping only the static guard; granting the reader records ; a CONNECTION LIMIT instead of a pool cap. Accepted risks Id Risk AR-TICK-549-01 (Low) Code inside the core process that deliberately opens a second pool with the reader DSN reads raw diffs. The guard and review cover it. AR-TICK-549-02 (Info) One more pool: up to 5 server connections per PgBouncer instance."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e1.html","title":"ADR-004-E1: cascade audit entries and diff_truncated","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED (rev 2), 2026-11-02, by the Solution Architect; nothing contested. Erratum to ADR-004 : replaces the R2 action list and the R1 envelope list, amends R23 and adds acceptance test CI-A17. Normative text: the E1 record ; explainer: E1-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\" items 1 and 2, \"Consequences\". Problem Two rows required by the cascade rules (ADR-001-A5 and the records-path plan) could not be expressed in ADR-004's vocabulary. Separately, a diff over 64 KB had no way to say it was cut. Decision Item 1, cascade entries (new R2a). action gains cascade_cycle and cascade_too_deep ; subject_type gains cascade_key . A cycle entry is written in the write's transaction, once per suppressed start. A too-deep entry is written on the pipeline's own transaction runner and commits independently of the rejected command. The cascade key is a fixed-namespace UUIDv5 over tenant, origin, record and definition, computed in Go by both the writer and the verifier. At most one cycle entry per origin, record and definition per write, so the unique key cannot roll back the user's write. Neither entry ever carries the attempted record data or an idempotency key. Item 2, diff_truncated . The envelope gains a boolean diff_truncated , always emitted, inside the chain, so flipping it is detectable. Past 64 KB the stored diff keeps whole RFC 6902 operations only; secrets are checked on the full diff before the cut. audit/erase entries are never truncated: the cap is at most 1,500 entry ids per erase entry, and an oversize one is refused, so erasure cannot silently leave entries that verify as tampered . No migration or backfill was needed; the table was empty when this was accepted."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e2.html","title":"ADR-004-E2: verifiability is computed, the trailer must agree","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-02 (rev 2), by the Solution Architect after the Technical Architect's re-check; nothing contested. Erratum to ADR-004 R20 and to the audit export format ( docs/api/audit-export-v1.md ). Normative text: the E2 record ; explainer: E2-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"Outcome table\". Problem R20 said an export made with stubs=false always reports verifiable: false . That judged some honest files tampered , for example a filtered window whose last entries were excluded. Decision The header's stubs field is the request, not a claim. It only decides whether every seq in the range must appear (a stubs: true file with a gap is tampered ). Only the trailer's verifiable must equal the computed value; a disagreement is tampered ( verifiable_flag_mismatch ). \"Computed\" includes the tail: the range is contiguous only if the anchor is present, no seq is skipped, and the last range line is last_seq . An honest stubs: false export that excluded the tail is unverifiable , not tampered . The exporter still streams: it counts as it goes and writes verifiable = (range non-empty) and (count + stub_count = last_seq - anchor_seq) . The verifier's reason codes become a closed list. Not a breaking change to v1: every file that passed still passes, and no exporter had shipped. The format stays nava.audit.export/v1 . A binding outcome table in the record maps header stubs , range shape and trailer flag to pass , unverifiable or tampered ."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e3.html","title":"ADR-004-E3: the commit-check probe is planned on every call","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-03, by the Solution Architect after all reviewers approved; the CTO ruled on one trade-off. Erratum to ADR-004 decision 2, R28 and CI-A3. Normative text: the E3 record ; explainer: E3-explained.html . Its CI verdict rule is later changed by E4 . Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\", \"Budget\". Problem The commit-check trigger probes audit_log once per subject at COMMIT. Under PostgreSQL's default plan caching, a pooled backend switches to a cached generic plan that is a sequential scan on a young partition and keeps it. A later import of thousands of subjects then costs quadratic time at COMMIT (2.9 s at 4,000 subjects), and the single-subject path decays too. Decision The commit-check function carries SET plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan , so every probe is planned with its own values and no plan outlives the call. A session setting cannot override it. The probe reads only plpgsql locals, never NEW.\u003ccol\u003e , so bound values appear as constants in the plan. CI-A3 checks the function's catalogue settings for the new option. The performance tests check the plan that actually runs at COMMIT, not a prepared generic plan. The budget row of 100 µs per call is replaced by two rows measured against 38 partitions: single subject must stay flat across sizes (max/min under 1.5) and import per probe has its own ceiling. Same-run ratio limits guard against runner noise. Rollout is one normal migration, before any table attaches the trigger. Scope is one function, one migration and one catalogue check; no schema, grant, policy, role or database setting changes."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e4.html","title":"ADR-004-E4: commit-check ceilings are report-only in CI","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-03, by the Solution Architect, wording the CTO's decision (Option B with four conditions); nothing contested. Erratum to ADR-004-E3 's Budget section. It is numbered E4 because E3 was already taken by the erratum it corrects. Normative text: the E4 record ; explainer: E4-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"Verdict table\". Problem On one commit, the same CI job read 129.5 µs and 190.9 µs on two GitHub-hosted runners against a 175 µs ceiling, with the same-run ratios unchanged. Absolute ceilings measured on shared hosts produced red builds that were not regressions. Decision The commit-check-budget job keeps measuring both rows as E3 defines them. Only the verdict rule changes. Absolute ceilings (per call and per import probe) become review thresholds: going over emits a warning annotation and the job stays green. The job fails on any of: a missing or non-positive measurement, a same-run ratio out of range, or a clause arm that is not flat across sizes. Every run logs its numbers and the host CPU model, and writes both rows to the step summary. Baselines in docs/budgets.md record the CPU model. The old \"re-run\" verdict is removed; that case is now a warning. Accepted risks E4-R1: a regression that slows the clause and the same-run reference equally keeps the ratios in range and passes. The cue to look was later reworded by ADR-004-E6 ."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e5.html","title":"ADR-004-E5: no FK actions on audited tables, no secrets in records","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-04, by the Solution Architect. Contested points were settled by CTO tie-breaks. Erratum to ADR-004 : adds rule R29, amends R6 for records and CI-A3. Normative text: the E5 record ; explainer: E5-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\" D1 to D11. Problem records.assignment_group_id was ON DELETE SET NULL from groups . When group sync deleted a group, PostgreSQL itself rewrote the records as the table owner, bypassing the write pipeline: no version bump, no audit entry, no outbox event. The commit-check trigger refused that write at COMMIT, so group sync, deprovisioning included, stalled whenever a record named the deleted group. Decision Tombstone groups. groups gains deleted_at . Sync never deletes a group row; a returning group is revived on the same platform id. The sync role loses DELETE on groups . R29. Every foreign key referencing an audited table must use NO ACTION or RESTRICT; CI checks it from the catalogue. records_assignment_group_fkey becomes RESTRICT . Readers. Sync's own diff read and the policy subquery see tombstoned rows; user-facing readers (such as /me group paths) filter them out. Admin and auditor pointers are deleted in the same transaction when their group is tombstoned, with matching narrow DELETE grants and policies. Pipeline. A write that changes assignment_group_id to a tombstoned group is refused with 422 assignment_group_removed ; an unchanged value passes. Records keep the reference and read models mark it removed: true . Database guard. A generated live column and composite foreign key make the database refuse members in a tombstoned group, for every role. No secrets in records. R6 applies to record payloads: a value at an x-nava-secret or writeOnly path is refused with 422 audit_secret_in_diff . There is no \"store and mask\" mode. Publishing a record definition that requires a marked property is refused. Migration order: expand first, contract only after the new binaries run everywhere."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e6.html","title":"ADR-004-E6: the E4-R1 cue is a per-model 1.2x band","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-05, by the Solution Architect after the Technical Architect approved with nothing contested. Erratum to the accepted risk E4-R1 in ADR-004-E4 only. It is numbered E6 because E1 to E5 were taken. Normative text: the E6 record ; explainer: E6-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\". Problem E4's cue to look for a uniform slowdown was an absolute warning. That is always on for a slow CPU model (one model's import baseline is already over the threshold) and blind on a fast one (a slowdown must reach more than twice the baseline before any warning). A spike showed timings on one CPU model are stable (max/min under 1.2). Decision A row is off-baseline when the run's CPU model has a recorded baseline in docs/budgets.md and the row is more than 1.2 times that model's recorded value. E4-R1 is revisited on two off-baseline main runs in a row on a recorded model, or a baseline re-take more than 1.2 times the previous one. Runs on unrecorded models neither count nor break the streak. An over-threshold baseline alone is not a regression signal; it is annotated as a slow host when its ratios match the other models. The check stays manual, owned by the Postgres specialist. A baseline re-take is the median of at least three runs on that model. Nothing in the CI gate changes: no verdict, threshold or code."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e7.html","title":"ADR-004-E7: A1 books the 10 s interval when work is pending","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-07, by the Solution Architect after the Technical Architect's critique and a CTO ruling on the path. It clarifies R16 of ADR-004 and changes nothing else; R-A2 is unchanged. Normative text: the E7 record ; explainer: E7-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\", \"Options considered\", \"Consequences\". Problem The sealer's first activity (A1) books the next run before the second (A2) seals. In the code, A1 used the interval that the previous run's A2 had stored, so every interval change landed one run late. After an idle period, entries written just after a run waited up to 300 s even though that run had sealed something, and a burst larger than one run's cap could wait about 600 s, breaking the documented bound and firing the sealing-lag alert on a healthy system. Decision R16's interval bullet is reworded: on the non-stale path and under the head lock, A1 checks whether the tenant has any unsealed entry. If so it books the next run 10 s ahead and stores 10 s; otherwise it books at the stored interval. A2 stores the interval for later runs (10 s if it sealed anything, otherwise doubled up to 300 s; unchanged on error). Bound, absent an A2 failure: an entry is sealed within the delay already booked when it was written (at most 300 s) plus one run's duration. A poison entry still blocks later entries; that is the failure-metric and chain:reseed path. Options that lost Rewording R16 to match the code and relaxing the bound to about 600 s; running A2 before A1 (ends the lineage on a crash); moving the next run's available_at forward from A2 (a new sealer write path and grant). Consequences No Temporal determinism impact: the change sits inside the A1 activity, so recorded histories replay as they are. Idempotency is unchanged."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e8.html","title":"ADR-004-E8: the reader also reads the chain columns","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-08, by the Solution Architect after the Technical Architect's critique and Security's approval on the PR; not contested. Erratum to ADR-004-A1 A1-1 and A1-7 only. It corrects an omission and changes no rule. Normative text: the E8 record ; explainer: E8-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\", \"Why these three columns are safe\". Problem A1 puts the export pages on the nava_audit_reader pool, and the export format needs the chain links and the erasure reference. But A1-1's column list omitted prev_hash , entry_hash and erasure_ref , so an export page failed with a permission error. A1 also did not say where the export's head sequence and hash come from. Decision The reader's audit_log grant gains prev_hash , entry_hash and erasure_ref , 30 columns in all. txid stays out. The three columns reveal nothing new: the hashes are derived from columns the reader already reads and are published by the export, and the erasure reference equals the matching erase entry's subject id. The head read for an export is the lease statement itself on nava_app , returning the head seq and entry_hash ; zero rows means 409 export_in_progress . The reader gets no grant on audit_chain_head . The lease transaction commits before the first page is read, so the head row lock is never held while paging."},{"url":"adr/adr-004.html","title":"ADR-004: one audit log, sealed by a per-tenant hash chain","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-02, by the Solution Architect after all three reviewers approved in round 2 (no dissent recorded). Normative text: ADR-004-audit-log.md ; explainer: ADR-004-explained.html . Supporting material: rfc/ , spikes/ . Amendment and errata: A1 , E1 , E2 , E3 , E4 , E5 , E6 , E7 , E8 . Each section names the ADR heading it paraphrases. Problem Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"Consequences\". The product needs one history of record changes and configuration changes that a buyer can export and check for tampering, under any filter, without trusting the operator's word. Decision Paraphrases: \"Decision\" 1 to 6. One log. A single append-only table, audit_log , with three domains: record , config and audit (exports, erasures, reseeds). One writer ( audit.Append , inside the caller's transaction), one reader and one export. The database checks the same-transaction rule. Every audited table gets a deferred constraint trigger. At COMMIT it requires an entry for the same tenant, transaction and subject. A raw write that skips audit.Append fails. Tamper evidence. A per-tenant hash chain, entry_hash = SHA-256(prev_hash ‖ envelope_sha256) , ordered by seq . A self-rescheduling sealer assigns seq asynchronously; writers never wait on it. Exports verify offline under any filter. Entries a filter excludes appear as hash-only stubs, and the range is anchored one entry before the window. The verifier ships as nava audit verify \u003cfile\u003e . Erasure is one of exactly two transitions allowed on a written row, and only the nava_erasure role may make it. The chain covers a salted diff hash, so it still verifies after erasure. S8's published versions are backfilled into the log by two migrations; they are not exposed through a view. Rules that matter most Paraphrases: \"Rules\", R1 to R28. Area What the ADR fixes Content (R1 to R4) The envelope columns, closed vocabularies for action , source and subject_type , versioned configuration with content hashes, and how AI and change-set actions are attributed. Secrets and masks (R5 to R8) One diff walker. A secret at an x-nava-secret or writeOnly path is rejected at write time. Masks are applied at read time. Reading needs the standalone audit.read or audit.export permission. Isolation and immutability (R9 to R12) FORCE row-level security, no DELETE grant for any role, INSERT asserted (never rewritten) by trigger, exactly two UPDATE transitions (seal, erase), writer grants generated from db/audited-tables.yaml . Chain and sealer (R13 to R18) The hash definition and golden vectors, one audit_chain_head row per tenant, seq rules, the self-rescheduling sealer, and chain:reseed (409 chain_alive unless the chain looks dead for 10 minutes). Export (R19 to R22) One export per tenant at a time, the anchored range, paging, JSONL and CSV formats with formula-prefix protection. Erasure (R23, R24) The erase step for Epic 30 and the verifier outcomes for erased entries ( tampered or pending unless a chained erase entry names them). Backfill and storage (R25 to R28) The order of migrations A and B, the access path for the backfill, lock order, and the partitioned key shape. Operations Operation Method and path Summary Responses listAuditEntries GET /api/v1/audit/entries The tenant's audit timeline 200, 400, 401, 403, 429, 500, 501 exportAuditEntries GET /api/v1/audit/entries:export Download a verifiable JSONL export of the audit log 200, 400, 401, 403, 409, 429, 500, 501 reseedAuditChain POST /api/v1/audit/chain:reseed Restart a dead audit seal lineage 200, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 429, 500, 501 Options that lost Paraphrases: \"Rejected options\". Separate record and configuration logs; a view over S8 versions instead of a backfill; chaining inside the writer's transaction; a shared seal key per minute (writers queued 3.9 to 4.0 s in two spikes); one seal start per transaction; an eternal per-tenant workflow; triggers that write the audit rows (they cannot know the actor or AI decision); jsonb diffs (the stored bytes would no longer match their hash); a single REPEATABLE READ export snapshot; external anchoring in the first milestone; a dedicated sealer role. Consequences Paraphrases: \"Consequences\". Each configuration epic registers its subject types and manifest rows. Entries carry sealed: false until the sealer ships. Sealing roughly doubles heap WAL on audit_log . The sealer needs an outbox producer entry point not tied to a record. Accepted risks Paraphrases: \"Accepted risks\". # Risk Revisit trigger R-A1 A consistent whole-chain rewrite by the owner is detected only against an export the tenant already holds. External anchoring was deferred. A regulated buyer asks for external attestation. R-A2 Sealing lag: about 10 s when the tenant is active, up to 300 s after idle. The audit_unsealed_oldest_seconds alert fires in production. R-A4 Commit-trigger, sealing-WAL and deferred-queue costs were unmeasured when accepted (spike S1). The S1 result. R-A5 Idle-tenant seal starts add Temporal load. Starts above 10% of Temporal capacity. R-A6 Migration B holds ACCESS EXCLUSIVE locks across its transaction. S8 volume at planning. (R8) A compromised worker credential can seal or insert for any tenant. Already accepted in ADR-001. The ADR-001 R8 trigger. Acceptance tests Paraphrases: \"Acceptance tests\", CI-A1 to CI-A16. They prove, among other things: no entry survives a rollback; a raw write to an audited table fails at COMMIT; read, export and reseed never cross tenants; export verification passes under every filter and fails on an edited byte, a deleted row or a deleted first entry; erasure verifies; the backfill leaves one entry per S8 version; a killed sealer is recovered by the next run."},{"url":"adr/adr-005.html","title":"ADR-005: record numbering","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-10, by the Solution Architect after the Technical Architect's critique and CTO rulings; no dissent. The ADR is accepted as a whole, but only part of it is built (see \"What is on main\"). Normative text: ADR-005-record-numbering.md ; explainer: ADR-005-explained.html . Supporting material: rfc/ , spikes/ . Delivery plan: plan-adr-005.md . Each section names the ADR heading it paraphrases. What is on main Part of the ADR State Per-type numbering_mode ( gaps default, gapless ) on entity definitions, and the allocator for both modes (story S10) Delivered on main . Scoped counters ( scope_key on record_counters , expand then contract migrations), release R1 (story S17) In progress, not on main . Number format tables, links to entity types, epochs, the admin API, formats rendered by the allocator, mandatory counter purge, and the contract migration (story S18) In progress, not on main . Field tokens (enum properties), immutable-field marker, coupled mask publish (story S19) In progress, not on main . Plugin-authored formats (story S20) Planned , parked until the plugin principal condition is met. Plugins that run code per create, or own the counter Deferred (per-create) and rejected (counter ownership). Until the scoped counters and formats ship, every type numbers with today's format, the type's prefix followed by seven digits, on the one counter row per tenant and type. Problem Paraphrases: \"Context\". Every record gets a human-facing number taken inside the create transaction, which keeps the counter row locked until the create commits. With 8 clients creating the same type in one tenant, counter wait p95 was 237 to 310 ms and create p95 264 to 408 ms against a 50 ms target. Tenants also want formats such as monthly or daily resets, or per-branch counters, and the format was fixed in code. Decision Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"2. The allocator\". Allocate in its own tiny transaction (L2s) on a dedicated allocator pool, after authorisation and validation and before the create transaction, with synchronous_commit off for that transaction only. This is the path for gaps types, the default. A create that fails after allocation leaves a hole; gaps are never reclaimed. gapless types take their number last, inside the create transaction (L1), so a rolled-back create gives its number back. The 50 ms 8-client target is waived for those types only. Both paths upsert the same counter row, so changing a type's mode cannot produce a duplicate number; the change applies to creates that start afterwards. Why L2s cannot put one number on two records: a record's synchronous commit flushes all earlier WAL, including the counter advance, so a crash can lose only advances whose numbers never reached a committed record. Number formats (planned, S17 to S19): declarative templates that start with the type's {prefix} , with exactly one {seq:N} , date parts, and enum field codes; versioned tenant metadata linked to entity types by pinned version. Scoped counters (planned): record_counters gains scope_key and a period end, with an expand, deploy, contract migration. The default format keeps today's counter, so no existing number is migrated. Options that lost Paraphrases: \"1. Options considered\", \"6. Plugins\". Keeping the counter lock until commit (misses the target); allocating on the main pool before authorisation (a caller with no create permission could advance a counter); cached blocks of numbers (large gaps on restart, kept as a trigger for very hot scopes); assigning the number after commit; a plugin that owns the counter or runs inside the lock (rejected, I8). Accepted risks Paraphrases: \"8. Accepted risks\". Id Risk AR-TICK-653-01 (Low) Numbers issued before a field was masked keep showing that field's code. AR-TICK-653-02 (Low) A per-scope sequence reveals volume per scope, within the tenant only. AR-TICK-653-03 (Low) The no-duplicate guarantee assumes WAL replays as a prefix; logical replication on record_counters reopens the ADR. AR-TICK-653-04 (Info) The allocator pool adds 4 connections per API replica. (product) Gaps on gaps types after failed creates or a crash. A tenant with a legal gapless requirement sets numbering_mode = gapless . Still owed Paraphrases: \"10. Still owed\". A one-client create over HTTP at or under 50 ms on a quiet host, and the matching budget rows."},{"url":"adr/index.html","title":"Architecture decisions","section":"adr","text":"An ADR records one decision and is normative. An amendment ( A\u003cn\u003e ) changes a rule of its parent ADR; an erratum ( E\u003cn\u003e ) corrects or clarifies one. Each page here summarises its record and links the normative text, which always wins. Pages marked in-progress describe work that is not yet on main . Record Title Status Date ADR-001 Platform blueprint — invariants, D26, D6, D22, D28 (system actors) ACCEPTED 2026-10-15 ADR-001-A1 Temporal server v1.29.7 / api v1.53.0, the D6-K startup guard and the D9 workload allow-list ACCEPTED 2026-10-20 ADR-001-A2 Erratum E2 and amendment A2 to ADR-001-A1, closing system-workflow and task-queue reach via service certificates (F2) ACCEPTED 2026-10-23 ADR-001-A5 Amendment A5 to ADR-001 I9 and CI-10, a cascade cycle suppresses the re-start (one start per tenant, origin, record and definition) instead of rejecting the write ACCEPTED 2026-10-31 ADR-001-A6 Amendment A6 to ADR-001 Annex A.2 and A.3 — the nava_erasure role, onboarding seeds the audit sealer, and start rows not tied to a record ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-001-E1 command inspection for continue-as-new ACCEPTED (inherited) 2026-10-15 ADR-001-E3 Erratum E3 to ADR-001 Annex A.3, nava_onboard has SELECT on its own row, not only its own provisioning row ACCEPTED 2026-10-25 ADR-002 GET /api/v1/me exposes the caller's group paths, tenant-scoped and with the tenant prefix stripped ACCEPTED 2026-10-27 ADR-002-E1 Erratum E1 to ADR-002, a line-scoped nolint pinned by a CI gate replaces the api.go forbidigo exclusion ACCEPTED 2026-10-28 ADR-003 A recorded interpreter history is retired only by raising GetVersion's minSupported (call kept), with an append-only tombstone CI can check ACCEPTED 2026-11-01 ADR-004 One audit log for records and configuration, sealed by a per-tenant hash chain and exportable (CR-AUDIT-1) ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-004-A1 Amendment A1 to ADR-004: only nava_audit_reader can read audit_log.diff and diff_salt, through the masking reader's own pool ACCEPTED 2026-11-04 ADR-004-E1 Erratum E1 to ADR-004, the R2 vocabulary and key shape for the two cascade audit entries, and diff_truncated in the R1 envelope ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-004-E2 Erratum E2 to ADR-004 R20 and audit-export-v1 §3/§4: only the trailer's verifiable must equal the computed value; header stubs is the request ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-004-E3 Erratum E3 to ADR-004 decision 2: the commit-check probe is planned on every call (force_custom_plan), Finding 5 tests the plan that runs, and the budget is rebased ACCEPTED 2026-11-03 ADR-004-E4 Erratum E4 to ADR-004-E3 (budget): in CI the absolute commit-check ceilings are report-only; the job fails only on a same-run ratio, on flatness, or when nothing was measured ACCEPTED 2026-11-03 ADR-004-E5 Erratum E5 to ADR-004: no referential actions on audited tables (group sync tombstones groups), and no secrets in record payloads ACCEPTED 2026-11-04 ADR-004-E6 Erratum E6 to ADR-004-E4 (accepted risk E4-R1): the uniform-regression cue is a per-model 1.2x band around the recorded baseline, not the absolute review threshold ACCEPTED 2026-11-05 ADR-004-E7 Erratum E7 to ADR-004 R16: A1 applies the 10 s interval when the tenant has unsealed entries; A2 stores the interval for later runs ACCEPTED 2026-11-07 ADR-004-E8 Erratum E8 to ADR-004-A1 A1-1 and A1-7: the reader also reads prev_hash, entry_hash and erasure_ref; the R20 head read is nava_app's lease statement ACCEPTED 2026-11-08 ADR-005 Record numbering — a number allocator outside the create transaction (L2s), declarative number formats linked to entity types, scoped counters ACCEPTED 2026-11-10 ADR-006 Filterable tenant fields — typed per-type side tables kept by trigger, late-flag backfill behind a snapshot barrier ACCEPTED 2026-11-12"},{"url":"benchmarks/index.html","title":"Benchmarks and budgets","section":"benchmarks","text":"A budget is a cost the platform keeps in bounds. docs/budgets.md is the normative list: what is measured, how, the limit, the baseline and the owner. A change that breaks a budget needs a new measurement and the owner's sign-off. This page puts each budget next to its latest result of record . How to read the tables One row per budget and host class. Results from different hosts cannot be compared. The commit-check trigger reads 80 µs per probe on one CI CPU model and 195 µs on another, in the same job. Host classes. CI runner is a GitHub-hosted ubuntu-latest runner, named with its CPU model. Idle reference machine is the Docker Desktop on Windows host with nothing else running. Reference host is the same kind of machine with other containers present. Laptop is a developer machine. Verdicts. met : within the limit. warn : over a report-only threshold; the CI job passes with a warning. waived and known miss : over the target, with the decision that accepts it linked in the row. no result yet : the budget exists but no measured result is committed. The row says \"no recorded result\" and is not left out. Commit. The commit the result was measured at. \"not recorded\" means the source did not record it. It is never guessed. Nothing is fetched. The figures come from docs/handbook/src/benchmarks/benchmarks.yaml , written by hand from the sources. Run links are plain links. The build fails when a row of docs/budgets.md has no entry there, or when a source file or heading no longer exists. Results Audit commit-check trigger (CI job commit-check-budget) Budget Threshold Latest result Verdict Date Commit Host class Evidence Commit-check trigger, single subject 300 µs per call at N = 200, 1,000 and 5,000 (warn). The job fails on fail_no_rows, fail_ratio or fail_flat (ADR-004-E4) 126.1 / 123.8 / 125.1 µs per call (max/min 1.02); reference 93.4, ratio 1.35 met 2026-11-03/04 not recorded CI runner (ubuntu-latest, 4 vCPU), AMD EPYC 9V45 source · run Commit-check trigger, single subject 300 µs per call (as above) 235.7 / 227.7 / 230.5 µs per call (1.03); reference 161.9, ratio 1.46 met 2026-11-03/04 not recorded CI runner (ubuntu-latest, 4 vCPU), AMD EPYC 7763 source · run Commit-check trigger, single subject 300 µs per call (as above) 170.5 / 163.6 / 164.8 µs per call (1.04); reference 133.5, ratio 1.28 met 2026-11-03/04 not recorded CI runner (ubuntu-latest, 4 vCPU), CPU not logged source · run Commit-check trigger, import (10,000 subjects) 175 µs per probe (warn). Same fail conditions as the single-subject row 80.3 µs per probe (runs 80.2 / 81.5 / 80.3); reference 9.9, ratio 8.13 met 2026-11-03/04 not recorded CI runner (ubuntu-latest, 4 vCPU), AMD EPYC 9V45 source · run Commit-check trigger, import (10,000 subjects) 175 µs per probe (as above) 194.9 µs per probe (runs 195.3 / 194.9 / 191.2); reference 22.7, ratio 8.58. Over the threshold on a slow host, not a regression: the ratio is in line with the other CPU models and the TICK-526 spike read 189.0 to 193.5 over 7 runs (ADR-004-E6). The job passes with a warning warn 2026-11-03/04 not recorded CI runner (ubuntu-latest, 4 vCPU), AMD EPYC 7763 source · run Commit-check trigger, import (10,000 subjects) 175 µs per probe (as above) 127.7 µs per probe (runs 127.4 / 127.7 / 129.3); reference 13.1, ratio 9.75 met 2026-11-03/04 not recorded CI runner (ubuntu-latest, 4 vCPU), CPU not logged source · run Migration lock holds Budget Threshold Latest result Verdict Date Commit Host class Evidence Migration A/B tenant-lock hold (S8 backfill, audit_backfill_s8()) ≤ 18 s at 100k S8 versions 0.18 ms per backfilled version, linear: 5.62 s at 30,000 and 54.69 s at 300,000 (first run); a re-run with every entry present takes 2.32 s at 300,000. 100k was not run: 18 s is the linear figure, and the limit was set from this measurement met 2026-11-03 not recorded Laptop (dev host, Windows 11, Docker, 16 CPUs, postgres:16.10) source Migration B lock hold (P5-32) ≤ 5 s at 10,000 tenants and 300,000 S8 versions 4.29 s at 10,000 tenants / 300,972 versions; 0.48 s at 1,000 / 30,093 met 2026-11-07 not recorded Laptop (dev host, Windows 11, Docker, 16 CPUs, postgres:16.10, fsync off) source Record history Budget Threshold Latest result Verdict Date Commit Host class Evidence Record history length (GET /api/v1/records/{entity}/{id}/history) 500 entries; over it the endpoint answers 422 history_too_long and reads nothing no recorded result. A cap enforced in code, not a timing: TestPatchHistoryIsCapped proves a page reads at most the cap's rows. Lifting the cap is TICK-548 no result yet - - None (no timing is recorded) source Record numbering and create (ADR-005, P3-36) Budget Threshold Latest result Verdict Date Commit Host class Evidence Counter wait, gaps type (L2s), 8 clients, SQL level ≤ 2 ms p95 0.99 ms p95 (p50 0.84) on a quiet host; 1.20 ms p95 on a contended host met 2026-11-09/10 not recorded Laptop (Technical Architect's spike S1 host, quiet), postgres:16.10 source Create, 1 client, in-process handler ≤ 50 ms p95 23.0 ms p95, first attempt, every gate passed met 2026-11-11 9975568b Idle reference machine (Docker Desktop on Windows, WSL2 disk; no PgBouncer, no HTTP transport) source Create, 8 clients, gaps type: agent / admin / small tenant ≤ 50 ms p95, kept strictly (option A) 42.4 / 42.1 / 47.2 ms p95, each row on its first attempt. Small tenant meets by 2.8 ms; whether production storage keeps the margin is an accepted risk met 2026-11-11 9975568b Idle reference machine (Docker Desktop on Windows, WSL2 disk; no PgBouncer, no HTTP transport) source · AR-S10-02 Waiver: create, 8 clients, gapless type (L1) ≤ 213.7 ms p95 (the 50 ms target is waived for gapless types only) 205.7 ms p95; counter wait p50 / p95 39.3 / 186.2 ms. Reported, not scored. docs/budgets.md still quotes the earlier 213.7 ms at 25aedcf0 (2026-11-10) as its baseline waived 2026-11-11 9975568b Idle reference machine (Docker Desktop on Windows, WSL2 disk; no PgBouncer, no HTTP transport) source · ADR-005 §2.1 L1 waiver Lists and reads at 800k records (P3-36) Budget Threshold Latest result Verdict Date Commit Host class Evidence List, fixed columns: agent by group page 1 / 20, agent filter[state]=new, admin state in ≤ 150 ms p95 73.5 / 71.2, 76.2, 68.2 ms p95 met 2026-11-10 9da6f7e1 Reference host (laptop, Docker on WSL2, 16 vCPU), idle apart from under-1% containers source List, other rows: agent sort=-updated_at / sort=assignee, admin page 1 / 20, requester page 1 / 20, small tenant agent / admin ≤ 150 ms p95 146.2 / 117.5, 66.6 / 59.8, 75.4 / 76.8, 111.5 / 70.4 ms p95. Some rows ran while another stack came up; contention only adds time, so a meet still holds met 2026-11-09 45635f89 Reference host (laptop, Intel i7-1260P, Docker on WSL2, 16 vCPU), partly contended source List, data filters: priority=1 / site=s7 ≤ 150 ms p95 223.8 / 224.4 ms p95; a timeout answers 422 filter_too_expensive, none hang known miss 2026-11-09 45635f89 Reference host (laptop, Intel i7-1260P, Docker on WSL2, 16 vCPU), partly contended source · accepted by the CTO (TICK-646) Single read: agent / admin / small tenant ≤ 50 ms p95 25.0 / 21.0 / 16.1 ms p95, no waiver met 2026-11-09 45635f89 Reference host (laptop, Intel i7-1260P, Docker on WSL2, 16 vCPU), partly contended source Other budgets Budget Threshold Latest result Verdict Date Commit Host class Evidence PostgreSQL server connections, compose stack at 1 nava-core replica ≤ 247 (max_connections 250 minus 3 reserved) 226, summed from the configured ceilings (computed, not measured); each further replica adds 24 met 2026-11-11 9975568b None: a static sum re-checked by TestConnectionBudgetFits on every test run source Relay claim latency (T33, task bench:relay, BenchmarkClaim), report-only None set: report-only no recorded result no result yet - - None source Where each number comes from Measurement How to run it Where it runs Commit-check trigger task test:commit-check-budget ( TestCommitCheckBudget ) CI job commit-check-budget , report-only (ADR-004-E4). The baseline is the median of at least 3 runs of the job on one CPU model (ADR-004-E6). A laptop run is not a baseline Lists, single reads and creates at 800k records (P3-36) task bench:acl-perf ( bench/acl-perf/run.sh ), about 32 minutes Manually, on the reference host; not in CI. Each create attempt counts only when it passes the transport, host-speed, storage and container gates Migration lock holds scratch/audit-backfill-s8/run.sh , docs/data/audit-migration-b-bench/run.sh Manually, on a developer host, against postgres:16.10 in Docker Connection sizing TestConnectionBudgetFits , TestAllocatorPoolSizing in task test Every test run: a static sum, nothing is timed Relay claim latency (T33) task bench:relay ( BenchmarkClaim ) Report-only; no result is committed Not on this page The 100/s open-loop create run (TICK-700) is informational: it has no target and no verdict. It is in acl-perf-800k.md . The PgBouncer load test (T06) checks correctness under load, not a latency budget: 50 clients on 5 server connections with zero prepared-statement errors. See pgbouncer-load.md . Production numbers. Every result here comes from a CI runner or a local Docker host. The go-live benchmark on production storage is the trigger of accepted risk AR-S10-02 in accepted-risks.md . Budget owners Section Budget Owner Commit-check trigger ( audit_commit_check , ADR-004 decision 2), single subject Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Commit-check trigger, import Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Migration lock holds Migration A/B tenant-lock hold (S8 backfill, audit_backfill_s8() ): ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on tenants , entity_definition_versions and audit_log until COMMIT Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Migration lock holds Migration B lock hold (P5-32): ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on tenants , entity_definitions , entity_definition_versions , audit_log , audit_chain_head and outbox until COMMIT (R27) Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Record history Record history length ( GET /api/v1/records/{entity}/{id}/history ) Principal Software Engineer Record numbering Counter wait, gaps type (L2s) Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Record numbering Create, 1 client, in-process handler Quality, Testing \u0026 CI Advisor Record numbering Create, 8 clients, gaps type Quality, Testing \u0026 CI Advisor Record numbering Waiver: create, 8 clients, gapless type (L1) Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist"},{"url":"database/index.html","title":"Database","section":"database","text":"TODO: tables by area, from the schema snapshot. Migrations Migration Purpose 00001_global_revokes.sql ADR-001 A.3 global settings. Runs as nava_migrator, the database owner. 00002_platform_tables.sql T09 (TICK-64): the platform tables of ADR-001 A.3, each under ENABLE + FORCE 00003_identity_tables.sql T16 (TICK-69): the identity tables group sync (T25) fills from Keycloak and 00004_tenant_email_domain.sql T18a (TICK-194): the email domains each tenant owns. InviteUser creates a 00005_purge_indexes.sql T13 follow-up (TICK-146): indexes for nava-purge's batch DELETEs 00006_pending_invite.sql T252a (TICK-254): the admins a tenant has invited who have not signed in 00007_email_domain_release.sql TICK-280 (follow-up from TICK-276 item 1, AR-036): nava_onboard may 00008_outbox_payload_references.sql T32c (TICK-214), CI-7 finding T32b-1 (docs/security/temporal-ci6.md): an 00009_outbox_payload_keys.sql T32f (TICK-240), CI-7 finding T32b-1 remainder (docs/security/temporal-ci6.md): 00010_principal_tombstone.sql TICK-220 (T25 follow-up): a principal who leaves the Keycloak directory is 00011_principal_tombstone_validate.sql TICK-220: validates 00010's principals_tombstone_check in its own 00012_drop_principals_delete_policy.sql TICK-265 (contract step of TICK-220): group sync tombstones a principal 20261028175712_idempotency_lease.sql T22a (TICK-212): a lease nava_app can extend, so a takeover of an abandoned 20261031150000_entity_definitions.sql P3-02 (TICK-340): entity types and their published, immutable versions 20261031160000_tenant_admin_group.sql P3-04 (TICK-342, PLAN-003 D-3-3): the platform id of each tenant's admins 20261101120000_tenant_admin_group_onboard_select.sql P3-04h (TICK-373, PLAN-003 D-3-3): \"active implies the tenant has an admin\" 20261101130000_entity_versions_published_by_check.sql P3-06c (TICK-383): every version after v1 names who published it, in the 20261101130100_entity_versions_published_by_validate.sql P3-06c (TICK-383): validates 20261101130000's 20261101180000_entity_definition_versions_jsonb_check.sql TICK-378: fixed_columns and states on entity_definition_versions must be 20261101180100_entity_definition_versions_jsonb_check_validate.sql TICK-378: validates the previous migration's fixed_columns and states 20261101190000_records_pipeline.sql P3-09 (TICK-353): records gains what the write pipeline needs, audit_log is 20261102100000_cascade_start.sql P3-12 (TICK-88): cascade_start, the start key of ADR-001 amendment A5 20261102120000_idempotency_response_headers.sql TICK-412: a replayed idempotent response restores an allow-listed set of 20261102120100_idempotency_response_headers_validate.sql TICK-412: validates the previous migration's response_headers CHECK in its 20261102130000_audit_log_v0.sql P5-10a (TICK-427), PLAN-005 D-5-1: S9's interim audit table becomes 20261102130100_tenant_auditor_group.sql P5-18 (TICK-434, PLAN-005 D-5-4): the platform id of each tenant's auditors 20261102233600_audit_log.sql P5-10b (TICK-450): ADR-004's audit_log, its chain head and the pipeline's 20261103100000_audit_commit_check.sql P5-11 (TICK-428): ADR-004 decision 2's commit check, as a function any 20261103120000_audit_backfill_s8.sql P5-15 (TICK-433): migration A's backfill (ADR-004 R25 A half, R26). Every 20261103150000_audit_commit_check_custom_plan.sql P5-11-E3 (TICK-508): audit_commit_check() is planned on every call 20261104013400_groups_tombstone.sql ADR-004-E5 step (i), expand (TICK-534; D1, D2, D5, D10, D11, I1, I5). Group 20261104013500_groups_tombstone_validate.sql ADR-004-E5 step (i) (TICK-534, I5): validates 20261104013400's 20261104043000_groups_contract.sql ADR-004-E5 step (ii-a), contract (TICK-538; D2, D10). Runs only after step 20261104043100_groups_contract_validate.sql ADR-004-E5 step (ii-b) (TICK-538; D10, CI-E5-8): validates 20261104043000's 20261104100000_drop_audit_log_v0.sql P5-17 (TICK-435): drops S9's audit_log_v0 (PLAN-005 D-5-1, moved here from 20261104110000_records_audit_commit_check.sql P5-13c (TICK-530): attach ADR-004 decision 2's commit check to records, 20261104133000_audit_reader_policies.sql ADR-004-A1 A1-3 (TICK-558): nava_audit_reader, the masking reader's own 20261104191200_auditor_group_active_tenant.sql P5-23 (TICK-568, ADR-004 with ADR-001-A6, PLAN-005 D-5-4): nava_onboard may 20261105100000_onboard_seal_start.sql P5-33 (TICK-441, ADR-001-A6.2, ADR-004 R16): onboarding seeds a tenant's 20261107053707_audit_migration_b.sql P5-32 (TICK-442): migration B (ADR-004 R25 B half, R26, R27; R16 20261107100000_audit_chain_head_dead_lineage.sql P5-34f (TICK-590, ADR-004 R17): the dead-lineage gauge. nava-purge, as 20261108120000_record_write_keys_actor.sql TICK-626 (AR-TICK-480-01, carried onto D-5-3): an idempotency key belongs 20261108120100_record_write_keys_actor_key.sql TICK-626 (AR-TICK-480-01): record_write_keys is unique per principal. 20261108124249_acl_tables.sql P3-30 (TICK-615): the S10 authorization tables (PLAN-003 D-3-2, the S10 20261108184658_record_shares_typed_principals.sql P3-30a (TICK-625, closes AR-S10-01): a record share names a principal of 20261109100000_acl_versions_shape_check.sql TICK-629 (deferred from P3-31): acl_versions.rules and masks must have 20261109100100_acl_versions_shape_check_validate.sql TICK-629: validates the previous migration's rules and masks CHECKs in 20261109120000_acl_versions_audit_commit_check.sql P3-35 (TICK-620): attach the audit commit check to acl_versions (ADR-004 20261109130000_audit_log_subject_acl_policy.sql P3-35 (TICK-620), contract amendment A3: registers the audit subject type 20261109130100_audit_log_subject_acl_policy_validate.sql P3-35 (TICK-620): validates the previous migration's audit_log subject_type 20261109140000_records_list_updated.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the default sort (-updated_at) and sort=updated_at. A 20261109140100_records_list_created.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): sort=created_at, read forward (descending) or backward 20261109140200_records_list_state.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): sort=state, ordered state, updated_at, id; and 20261109140300_records_list_group.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the access path for assignment_group_id: 20261109140400_records_list_requester.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the access path for requester_id: filter[requester]=\u003cp\u003e 20261109140500_records_list_assignee.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the access path for assignee_id: filter[assignee]=\u003cp\u003e and 20261109140600_drop_records_s9_list_indexes.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): drops P3-09's list indexes, which the records_list_* 20261109140700_platform_database_jit_off.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): JIT off for every session on the platform database. 20261110040000_entity_definitions_numbering_mode.sql P3-36f (TICK-659): each entity type chooses how its records are numbered 20261110040100_entity_definitions_numbering_mode_validate.sql P3-36f (TICK-659): validates entity_definitions_numbering_mode_check, added 20261110100000_records_list_group_state.sql TICK-656 (P3-36d): the group access path with state as an index condition."},{"url":"database/schema.html","title":"Schema snapshot (schema.json)","section":"database","text":"Schema snapshot: schema.json docs/handbook/src/database/schema.json describes the platform database schema as a freshly migrated database has it. The handbook build ( docs/handbook/build/build.py ) reads this file. It never parses the migration SQL and never connects to a database (TICK-718 D-HB-9, TICK-733). This page is the contract between the writer (TICK-733) and the handbook reader (TICK-719), as the Technical Lead decided on TICK-733. Regenerating it The snapshot is generated, never hand-edited. Regenerate it after a migration lands on main , and commit it with the handbook update for that change. Start the local stack ( docker compose up ), so that the migrate one-shot has migrated the platform database nava . The migrator also builds the runtime audit_log policies from db/audited-tables.yaml . From the repository root, run the one command: docker compose exec -T postgres sh -c 'PGPASSWORD=\"$(cat /run/pg_superuser_password)\" psql -h /var/run/postgresql -U pgadmin -d nava -XAtq -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1' \u003c docs/handbook/build/schema.sql | python3 docs/handbook/build/schema_json.py docs/handbook/src/database/schema.json On Windows, use python instead of python3 if that is your interpreter's name. The two build files do the following: docs/handbook/build/schema.sql reads the catalogue and prints one JSON document. Every name is sorted with COLLATE \"C\" (byte order), so the output does not depend on the database's default collation. docs/handbook/build/schema_json.py uses only the Python standard library. It checks the document against the key table below: exact keys per object, kind values, the partition-key rules, and byte order. It refuses a table outside schema public or a foreign table, and it refuses connection details (see the last section). It writes the file only when every check passes. Version 1 The file is UTF-8 with LF line endings, a two-space indent and one trailing newline. Non-ASCII characters are written as-is, not escaped. Every key below is always present: a string that does not apply is \"\" , and a list that is empty is [] . A reader ignores keys it does not know, so a new key is additive and does not change version . Renaming or removing a key bumps version . The snapshot covers schema public only. Tables are named by their bare name. goose_db_version , the migrator's bookkeeping table, is left out. Key Value version 1 . A reader rejects any other value. tables Every table in public , sorted by name . tables[].name The table name. tables[].kind Exactly one of table , partitioned or partition . tables[].partition_of A partition's parent table, else \"\" . tables[].partition_by A partitioned table's key ( pg_get_partkeydef ), for example RANGE (occurred_at) , else \"\" . tables[].partition_bound A partition's bound ( pg_get_expr(relpartbound) ), for example DEFAULT , else \"\" . tables[].rls Row level security flags. tables[].rls.enabled ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY. tables[].rls.forced FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY. tables[].columns Columns in ordinal ( attnum ) order. tables[].columns[].name Column name. tables[].columns[].type format_type , for example timestamp with time zone . tables[].columns[].nullable true unless the column is NOT NULL. tables[].columns[].default The deparsed default expression, else \"\" . tables[].primary_key Primary key columns in key order, [] when there is no primary key. tables[].foreign_keys Foreign keys sorted by name . A partition lists its own copies of its parent's. tables[].foreign_keys[].name Constraint name. tables[].foreign_keys[].columns Referencing columns in key order. tables[].foreign_keys[].ref_table Referenced table. tables[].foreign_keys[].ref_columns Referenced columns in key order. tables[].foreign_keys[].on_delete NO ACTION , RESTRICT , CASCADE , SET NULL or SET DEFAULT . tables[].indexes Indexes sorted by name , including the primary key's. A partition lists its own copies of its parent's. tables[].indexes[].name Index name. tables[].indexes[].method Access method, for example btree or gin . tables[].indexes[].unique true for a unique index. tables[].indexes[].columns Key columns in order. An expression index's entry is the deparsed expression. tables[].indexes[].include The INCLUDE columns, [] when there are none. tables[].indexes[].predicate A partial index's deparsed WHERE , else \"\" . tables[].policies pg_policies rows sorted by name . tables[].policies[].name Policy name. tables[].policies[].command SELECT , INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE or ALL . tables[].policies[].roles Roles, sorted. tables[].policies[].permissive true for PERMISSIVE, false for RESTRICTIVE. A RESTRICTIVE policy changes what isolation means, so the sensitivity check must see it. tables[].policies[].using The deparsed USING , else \"\" . tables[].policies[].check The deparsed WITH CHECK , else \"\" . The predicates are PostgreSQL's own deparse, so the tenant predicate reads exactly as catalog.TenantPred(\"tenant_id\") . Not in version 1: check and unique constraints, sequences, functions, triggers, grants, owners, comments and table areas. Grants come from db/grants.yaml , audited or exempt status from db/audited-tables.yaml , and the table-to-area mapping from docs/handbook/src/database/areas.yaml on the handbook side. What it never contains The file holds no DSN, host, port, role password, pg_hba.conf address or local path. Version 1 has no key that could carry one. schema_json.py refuses to write a file that matches any of its leak patterns, and HB-12's manual sensitivity sweep covers the file. A future version that adds a free-text key, such as comments or owners, needs a new sensitivity review first."},{"url":"glossary/index.html","title":"Glossary","section":"glossary","text":"Terms used across the handbook, including every term that appears in a vision-section heading. Each entry points to the page that explains it most fully. Pages for the security, database and workflow sections are written by other handbook tasks; until they exist, entries point to the section's index. Tenancy and identity Tenant. One customer organisation using the shared platform. Main page: I1: tenant wall . Tenant wall. The row-level-security rule set that makes a query without a tenant return nothing. I1 . Row-level security (RLS) and FORCE RLS. A PostgreSQL feature that adds an invisible \"only this tenant's rows\" filter to every query. FORCE applies it even to the table's owner. I1 . Principal. The authenticated caller of an API request, a user or a service, resolved from a verified token inside one tenant. I2 . Group. A set of principals in one tenant, changed only through the platform API (tenants cannot administer Keycloak in Milestone 1, residual R3) and mirrored from Keycloak into the platform's identity tables by the group sync ( internal/groupsync ); used by access rules. I5 . Identity port and adapter. The core-owned interface to the identity provider and the single package that talks to Keycloak. I8 . Platform-verified source. A tenant value that comes from a validated token via the identity binding table, or from a row the platform wrote. I2 . Database roles Database role and least privilege. One narrow database account per system actor; none is a superuser, owns objects or bypasses RLS. I3 . Grant manifest. The single file listing every permission of every role; CI compares the live database with it. I3 . Migrator. The one-shot job that owns the schema and runs migrations. Database . Records and writes Entity definition and entity definition version. A tenant's JSON Schema description of a kind of record, and one published, immutable version of it. Database . Record. One stored item of an entity type, with a version used for optimistic concurrency. I4 . Write pipeline. The single path every write takes: authorisation, defaults, schema validation, checks, commit with audit. I4 . Optimistic concurrency. An update names the version it expects; a stale one is a typed conflict (HTTP 412 with If-Match ). I4 . Authorization layer. The access rules, compiled to SQL and agreeing with the Go single-record check. I5 . ACL and ACL version. An access-control list for an entity type, and the version number of the published list a decision was made under. I5 . Mask (field mask). A rule hiding a field from readers who may not see it, on write and on read. I5 . Numbering mode. How a record's human-readable number is allocated, per tenant, from a counter table. I8 . Idempotency key. A client-supplied key ( Idempotency-Key ) that makes a repeated write return the first response. I7 . Idempotent. Doing it twice has the effect of doing it once. I7 . Audit Audit log. The per-tenant record of every change, written in the same transaction as the change. Database . Seal. The sequence number and hash assigned to an audit entry by the asynchronous sealer. Database . Chain head. The latest sealed entry of a tenant's audit hash chain; each new seal hashes the previous one. Database . Workflows Outbox. A table of work rows saved in the same transaction as the change that caused them. I6 . Relay. The process that reads outbox rows and starts or signals workflows. I6 . Temporal and workflow. The workflow engine and a durable process it runs. Workflow code is deterministic; side effects run in activities. I6 . Causation and cascade. The origin event id and depth carried by every write and outbox row; a cascade is a write that triggers further writes, bounded by a depth limit. I9 . Process words Invariant. A rule that must always hold; changing one needs a new signed ADR. The nine invariants . Decision (D and G numbers). D numbers are decisions in ADR-001 section 2; G numbers are the TICK-2 gaps it closes (G7 is recorded as a decision). Goals and non-goals . Goal and non-goal. What Milestone 1 sets out to do, and what it deliberately leaves out. Goals and non-goals . Configuration, not code. Tenant changes are data, never new code or schema changes. I8 . Advisory AI. AI may suggest, and change data only above a recorded confidence threshold. I8 . Proof and enforcement point. The test or ADR section that shows an invariant holds, and the layer that applies it. The nine invariants . Known technical risks. The CTO's register of live risks. Vision . ADR. Architecture Decision Record. Architecture decisions ."},{"url":"index.html","title":"Nava handbook","section":"home","text":"Orientation for engineers: what the platform is, how it is built, and where each part lives. Every page describes only what is on main ; anything on a story branch is marked \"in progress\". TODO: sections overview and reading order (written by the content tasks of TICK-718). Vision System Architecture decisions Stories Workflows Database Security Benchmarks Operations Glossary"},{"url":"operations/index.html","title":"Operations","section":"operations","text":"How to run the platform on one machine and see what it is doing. Everything here is the local Docker Compose stack in compose.yaml . There is no production deployment on main yet. Page What it covers Local stack Start, stop, reset and seed the stack; what each service is and which ports reach the host Unpublished listeners Admin endpoints, the onboarding API and the Temporal UI publish no port. How to reach them through a tunnel or docker compose exec Traces, logs and metrics OpenObserve and Prometheus: finding a slow or failed request, alert rules, health endpoints Troubleshooting Where to look first when something breaks Operator tools Tenant onboarding goes through nava-onboard , an internal API that is never published. The request, its validation and how to recover a half-provisioned tenant are in docs/operator/onboarding.md . The cursor keyring is in docs/operator/cursor-keyring.md . nava audit verify \u003cfile\u003e checks an audit export ( nava.audit.export/v1 , JSONL or CSV) with no database and no network. task build writes it to bin/nava . Its exit codes are a contract: 0 every line verified, 1 tampered (the first failing line is reported), 2 usage error, 3 an erasure entry is not sealed yet, 4 unverifiable, 5 nothing sealed in the window. The audit chain itself is on the ADR-004 page . Commands at a glance Command What it does task stack:up make dev-ca , then docker compose --profile operator up -d --build task stack:down docker compose --profile operator down -v : stops the stack and deletes its volumes task stack:test Runs every stack case in deploy/compose/test/cases against a running stack task demo:outbox The S7 demo: one record written through the pipeline, one workflow started and signalled twice make operator-tunnel Forwards 127.0.0.1:18233 to the Temporal UI until interrupted sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh up Forwards for every unpublished listener (see Unpublished listeners ) sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh Seeds the two demo tenants and runs one group sync. Idempotent"},{"url":"operations/listeners.html","title":"Unpublished listeners","section":"operations","text":"Only nava-core ( 127.0.0.1:18080 ), Keycloak ( 127.0.0.1:18081 ), OpenObserve ( 127.0.0.1:5080 ) and Prometheus ( 127.0.0.1:9091 ) publish a port. Everything below listens only inside the stack's networks, on purpose. Reach it with a local tunnel or from inside a container. What is unpublished Listener Serves Why it stays off the host Admin :9090 on nava-core , nava-worker , nava-relay , nava-purge , nava-onboard , nava-groupsync ( NAVA_ADMIN_ADDR ) /metrics , /healthz , /readyz /metrics has no authentication of its own. Anything that reaches :9090 can read it. :8080/metrics on nava-core answers 404 nava-onboard:8081 The operator onboarding API The only holder of the onboarding secret and the nava_onboard database URL temporal-ui:8080 The Temporal web UI, as nava-operator It acts with the operator's certificate postgres:5432 , pgbouncer:5432 , temporal:7233 Database, pooler, Temporal gateway Platform traffic only Tunnels Each tunnel is a short-lived container of the nava/temporal-server image running its TCP proxy , bound to 127.0.0.1 on the host. Command Forwards make operator-tunnel 127.0.0.1:18233 ( UI_PORT ) to temporal-ui:8080 , until Ctrl+C. Needs the operator profile sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh up 127.0.0.1:18082 to nava-onboard:8081 ; 127.0.0.1:19090 to nava-core:9090 ; 19091 to 19095 to the admin listeners of nava-onboard , nava-groupsync , nava-relay , nava-worker and nava-purge , in that order; 127.0.0.1:18233 to the Temporal UI when it runs sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh status / down Lists or removes those forwards The tunnels stop when the stack is recreated: run tunnels.sh up again. Close them when you are done. While a tunnel runs, any container on a non-internal network can reach its target through the host. For the Temporal UI that means acting as nava-operator ( TODO-006 in make/temporal-server.mk ). Tunnels are for a local dev stack only. Without a tunnel Run the request from inside the container. The services' images carry wget : docker compose exec nava-core wget -qO- http://127.0.0.1:9090/readyz docker compose exec nava-relay wget -qO- http://127.0.0.1:9090/metrics The onboarding API is called the same way, with the operator token passed through the environment rather than the command line; the full request is in docs/operator/onboarding.md . Under the no-egress overlay ( deploy/compose/compose.internal.yaml ) this is the only way in. Health endpoints Endpoint Meaning Use it for /readyz Ready to serve. On nava-core only PostgreSQL gates it Readiness checks, the compose health checks /healthz A report: {status, checks} per dependency (PostgreSQL, Keycloak, Temporal). 503 while PostgreSQL is down; a failing Keycloak or Temporal shows as degraded Reading why a service is unhappy. Not a liveness probe: a restart policy on it turns a database outage into a restart loop /metrics Prometheus text format Prometheus scrapes it over internal"},{"url":"operations/local-stack.html","title":"Local stack","section":"operations","text":"The whole platform runs from compose.yaml on one machine with Docker. Every image is pinned ( docs/pins.md ). Passwords in compose.yaml are dev-only defaults so a fresh clone needs no setup; override them in .env (git-ignored, start from .env.example ). Never reuse them anywhere else. Start From the repository root: cp .env.example .env # once; optional, every value has a default task stack:up # make dev-ca, then docker compose --profile operator up -d --build docker compose ps # wait until every long-running service says healthy sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh # two demo tenants and one group sync (idempotent) make dev-ca writes the dev certificate authority and the Temporal mTLS certificates to deploy/compose/certs/ (git-ignored). It keeps existing certificates; FORCE=1 make dev-ca reissues them. task stack:up starts the operator profile too: the Temporal UI, OpenObserve and Prometheus. Plain docker compose up -d leaves those three out. First boot takes a few minutes: Keycloak imports its realm, and the one-shot jobs ( db-roles , migrate , temporal-schema ) must finish before the services that depend on them start. docker compose up --wait blocks until everything is healthy. To keep every trace instead of the default 10 %, start with OTEL_TAIL_SAMPLE_PERCENT=100 task stack:up . Errors and requests slower than 500 ms are always kept. Stop and reset Goal Command Stop, keep data docker compose --profile operator stop Stop and delete every volume (tenants, users, workflows, telemetry) task stack:down Full reset for the acceptance guide sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh down , docker compose down -v , docker compose up --wait , then seed.sh and tunnels.sh up again Re-seed only sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh What runs Service Role Networks Reaches the host postgres The one PostgreSQL 16 for the platform, Keycloak and Temporal internal , temporal-backplane No. The superuser logs in only over the Unix socket db-roles , migrate One-shot: roles and grants from db/grants.yaml , then goose migrations none / internal No pgbouncer Transaction-mode pooler in front of the nava database internal No keycloak Identity provider, realm nava default , internal 127.0.0.1:18081 ( KEYCLOAK_PORT ) temporal-schema , temporal-server , temporal Schema job, the mTLS Temporal server, and the gateway that forwards 7233 only temporal-backplane ; the gateway also internal and operator No temporal-ui Temporal web UI, profile operator operator No: tunnel to 127.0.0.1:18233 otel-collector Receives OTLP traces and logs, tail-samples, forwards to OpenObserve internal No openobserve Trace and log search, profile operator internal , operator UI on 127.0.0.1:5080 ( OPENOBSERVE_PORT ) prometheus Scrapes every admin /metrics , evaluates alert rules, profile operator internal , operator UI on 127.0.0.1:9091 ( PROMETHEUS_PORT ) nava-core The public API default , internal 127.0.0.1:18080 ( NAVA_CORE_PORT ) nava-worker , nava-relay , nava-purge , nava-groupsync Workflow worker, outbox relay, retention and seal checks, Keycloak group sync internal No nava-onboard Operator onboarding API on :8081 internal No: tunnel or exec Every published port binds 127.0.0.1 only. Each component has its own page in the system section . Variants Ports taken. Set NAVA_CORE_PORT , KEYCLOAK_PORT and KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:\u003cKEYCLOAK_PORT\u003e in .env together. A token's issuer must match KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL , or every call answers 401. No egress. docker compose -f compose.yaml -f deploy/compose/compose.internal.yaml up -d makes every network internal. Published ports stop working; use docker compose exec . A second stack on the same host ( COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME , NAVA_IMAGE_TAG and the port variables) is what the isolated CI runs use; see docs/quality/ci-pipeline.md . Group sync on demand: docker compose run --rm --no-deps nava-groupsync --once runs one cycle instead of waiting for NAVA_GROUPSYNC_INTERVAL (default 5 minutes). Check that it works task stack:test runs every case under deploy/compose/test/cases/ against the running stack. One case: sh deploy/compose/test/stack-test.sh \u003cname\u003e . Start the stack with OTEL_TAIL_SAMPLE_PERCENT=100 for the trace case. task demo:outbox writes a record of the seeded tenant-a through the write pipeline and prints the workflow id it started. Open it in the Temporal UI (namespace nava ) through make operator-tunnel . The acceptance guide walks each delivered story with Bruno: docs/acceptance/guide/index.html . Git Bash on Windows MSYS rewrites a leading-slash container path in a docker argument. The repository's scripts and make targets set MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1 themselves. A docker run , create or exec you type with a container path must set it too."},{"url":"operations/observability.html","title":"Traces, logs and metrics","section":"operations","text":"Every nava service emits three signals. Locally, all of them land in two browser UIs that task stack:up starts (profile operator ). Signal Leaves the service as Lands in Open it at Traces OTLP/HTTP push to otel-collector , tail-sampled OpenObserve, Traces view http://127.0.0.1:5080 Logs slog lines on stdout, and OTLP to otel-collector OpenObserve, Logs view, stream default ; also docker compose logs same Metrics Prometheus text on the admin listener :9090/metrics Prometheus, scraped over internal http://127.0.0.1:9091 Log in to OpenObserve with OPENOBSERVE_USER and OPENOBSERVE_PASSWORD . Both have dev-only defaults; see .env.example . Find one request Every signal carries the same ids: From To Link Client Everything The X-Request-Id response header is request_id in the logs and nava.request_id on the span. An error body's instance is urn:nava:request:\u003cid\u003e Log line Trace trace_id and span_id on every request log line Metric Trace Exemplars on duration buckets carry a trace_id (Prometheus runs with exemplar storage on) In OpenObserve's Logs view, in the SQL box: request_id = '\u003crequest id\u003e' -- one request trace_id = '\u003c32-hex trace id\u003e' -- one trace severity = 'ERROR' -- by level Free text: type a word in the search box, or match_all('timeout') . Expand a log line and choose View Trace to open its trace. In the Traces view, pick service nava-core and the time range. Why is this request slow? Open its trace. The spans show each database round trip and each call to Keycloak or Temporal with its duration. The P3-36 work on the benchmarks page reads creates exactly this way. Sampling The Collector keeps 10 % of ordinary traces ( OTEL_TAIL_SAMPLE_PERCENT ). Errors and requests slower than 500 ms are always kept. Set OTEL_TAIL_SAMPLE_PERCENT=100 when you start the stack to keep everything. If OpenObserve is not running (a stack without the operator profile), the Collector logs a failed export for each batch, drops it after 5 s of retries and stays healthy. Metrics and alerts Targets. Prometheus, Status \u003e Targets, job nava : the admin listeners of nava-core, nava-worker, nava-relay, nava-groupsync, nava-purge and nava-onboard. A target that is down means the service is down or its admin listener is unreachable. Labels are bounded. Metrics carry the HTTP method, status, route pattern (never the raw path), scheme, protocol and error.type . Tenant and user ids are never labels: to find the tenant, follow the log line. Alert rules live in deploy/compose/prometheus/rules/ . There is no Alertmanager locally, so a firing alert shows on Prometheus's Alerts page. Alert Fires when Where to look AuditSealLag (warning) An audit entry has been unsealed for more than 600 s audit_seal_failures_total and the nava-worker audit seal failed log line, which names the tenant AuditDeadLineage (critical) A seal lineage is more than 10 minutes overdue The nava-purge audit seal lineage dead log line. Recovery is POST /api/v1/audit/chain:reseed AuditDeadLineageCheckDown (warning) The dead-lineage check has reported nothing for 5 minutes The nava-purge audit dead lineage check failed log line: a lost grant, a dropped policy or a renamed column What is never in telemetry Spans carry no client values or personal data. Logs carry no raw path, query, headers, client IP or tokens. The full list of controls and known gaps is in observability-notes.md ."},{"url":"operations/troubleshooting.html","title":"Troubleshooting","section":"operations","text":"Where to look first, by symptom. The acceptance guide's troubleshooting page covers the Bruno walk-through in more detail. First three looks docker compose ps : is every long-running service healthy ? A one-shot job ( db-roles , migrate , temporal-schema ) that exited non-zero blocks everything after it. docker compose logs --since 5m \u003cservice\u003e : the reason is usually on one line. Every error response's instance is urn:nava:request:\u003cid\u003e ; search the logs for that id. /healthz of the service, through a tunnel or exec : it names the failing dependency. The stack does not start Symptom Cause Fix ca.pem: no such file No dev certificates yet make dev-ca ( task stack:up runs it) all predefined address pools have been fully subnetted Old Docker networks docker network prune (removes unused networks only) A port is already in use Another stack or program on 18080, 18081, 5080 or 9091 Set NAVA_CORE_PORT , KEYCLOAK_PORT with KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL , OPENOBSERVE_PORT or PROMETHEUS_PORT in .env Startup takes minutes Keycloak imports its realm on first boot; one-shot jobs run first Normal on a busy machine. docker compose up --wait keeps waiting Calls fail Symptom Cause Fix 401 invalid_token on a fresh token The token's issuer is not KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL/realms/nava : Keycloak was reached through another host or port Use the shipped URLs, or change KEYCLOAK_PORT and KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL together and restart 401 invalid_token after a while Access tokens live 5 minutes Sign in again 401 tenant_unresolved The tenants are not seeded, or no group sync has run since sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh 403 forbidden on /api/v1/operator/tenants A tenant token was sent Use the operator sign-in (client nava-operator-cli ) Connection refused on 18082 , 19090 to 19095 or 18233 The tunnels are not running; they stop when the stack is recreated sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh up 18233 only The Temporal UI is not started Start with the operator profile ( task stack:up ), then the tunnels again 502 from Keycloak Keycloak is still importing its realm Wait for docker compose ps keycloak to say healthy Every token refused ( 400 unauthorized_client ) after PostgreSQL restarted Keycloak's database connections died with it Keycloak validates its pool on every borrow, so this should recover on its own; nava-core's /healthz keycloak check shows fail until it does Something is slow or stuck Symptom Where to look One request is slow Its trace in OpenObserve, by request_id ( Traces, logs and metrics ) A workflow did not start after a write nava-relay logs and its admin /metrics ; the workflow in the Temporal UI (namespace nava ) Audit entries are not sealed Prometheus Alerts: AuditSealLag , AuditDeadLineage , AuditDeadLineageCheckDown Users or groups changed in Keycloak do not show Group sync runs every 5 minutes; run one cycle with docker compose run --rm --no-deps nava-groupsync --once A tenant is stuck in provisioning docs/operator/onboarding.md , \"Steps and recovery\" Start over sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh down docker compose down -v # deletes all data: tenants, users, workflows, telemetry docker compose up --wait sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh up"},{"url":"security/index.html","title":"Security","section":"security","text":"TODO: tenant isolation, authentication and the threat model."},{"url":"stories/index.html","title":"Stories","section":"stories","text":"Every story, with its status on main , the verdict its acceptance kit records, and the date. The table is generated from docs/handbook/src/stories/stories.yaml . Only delivered stories are on main ; in-progress stories live on a story branch and planned ones have no branch yet. Story Issue Title Status Verdict Date S1 TICK-43 Local stack starts healthy and CI gates every PR delivered READY 2026-10-25 S2 TICK-44 A query without the right tenant returns nothing delivered READY 2026-10-26 S3 TICK-45 A tenant user signs in and is scoped to their tenant only delivered READY 2026-10-27 S4 TICK-46 An operator onboards a tenant and its admin signs in delivered READY 2026-10-28 S5 TICK-47 Group changes and deprovisioning take effect within 10 minutes delivered READY 2026-10-27 S6 TICK-48 API ground rules on the first endpoints delivered READY 2026-10-29 S7 TICK-49 A committed outbox row runs exactly one tenant-scoped workflow delivered READY 2026-10-27 S8 TICK-333 A tenant admin publishes an incident type and reads every version delivered READY 2026-11-01 S9 TICK-335 An agent logs an incident, updates it, sees its history; a stale update is refused delivered READY 2026-11-02 S10 TICK-336 A service-desk agent sees only their group's incidents; masked fields stay hidden delivered READY 2026-11-11 S11a TICK-334 The workflow interpreter runs a definition on Temporal (scope: interpreter only; S11b, records starting workflows, is in progress) delivered READY 2026-11-01 S13 TICK-418 An auditor verifies an audit export offline, and any tampering is detected delivered READY 2026-11-02 S14 TICK-419 Every record and entity-type change lands in one audit log delivered READY 2026-11-04 S15 TICK-420 Each tenant's audit entries are sealed into a hash chain delivered READY 2026-11-07 S16 TICK-421 An auditor exports a filtered audit log and proves it complete delivered READY 2026-11-08 S11b TICK-337 Creating an incident starts a workflow that waits (story branch TICK-337/workflows-on-records) in-progress - - S17 TICK-664 Record numbers keep counting while the counter table moves (story branch TICK-664/counter-scope-key) in-progress - - S12 TICK-338 Agents comment, write internal work notes, and link records planned - - S18 TICK-669 A tenant admin defines a number format and links it to a ticket type planned - - S19 TICK-678 A record number can carry a branch or other enum code planned - - S20 TICK-684 A plugin authors number formats through the public API (parked) planned - - Kits and folders that belong to no single story: Path Reason docs/acceptance/guide/bruno/X1 API suite for getMe, spans S2 to S6; not one story docs/acceptance/guide/bruno/X2 API suite for onboardTenant, spans S4, S6 and S8 docs/acceptance/guide/bruno/X3 API suite for every admin listener, spans S1 and the services docs/acceptance/guide/bruno/X4 API suite for entity types, spans S8 and S14 docs/acceptance/guide/bruno/X5 API suite for records, spans S9, S10 and S14 docs/acceptance/guide/bruno/Z expired-token check that runs last, across every story docs/acceptance/guide/acceptance-run.md the recorded Bruno run over the whole stack (S1 to S8 and the API suite), not one story docs/acceptance/regression-milestone-1.md regression of milestone 1 (S1 to S8) on main, not one story docs/acceptance/acceptance-runs-tick287-329.md the earlier Postman run over the whole stack, superseded by the Bruno run Story pages Each page says what a user can now do, shows the flow, lists the API operations, says how to try it (Bruno requests, seed.sh , tunnels.sh ) and links the verdict and evidence. The step-by-step kit stays in the acceptance guide . S1: Local stack starts healthy and CI gates every PR S2: A query without the right tenant returns nothing S3: A tenant user signs in and is scoped to their tenant only S4: An operator onboards a tenant and its admin signs in S5: Group changes and deprovisioning take effect within 10 minutes S6: API ground rules on the first endpoints S7: A committed outbox row runs exactly one tenant-scoped workflow S8: A tenant admin publishes an incident type and reads every version Pages for S9 and later are not written yet."},{"url":"stories/s1.html","title":"S1: Local stack starts healthy and CI gates every PR","section":"stories","text":"Story TICK-43. Verdict READY , 2026-10-25, recorded in the acceptance kit . What a user can now do Bring up the whole platform on one machine with one command: PostgreSQL, PgBouncer, Keycloak, Temporal, the Collector, nava-core and the worker. Ask the service whether it and its dependencies are well. It stays ready when only Keycloak or Temporal is down, and stops taking traffic within seconds when the database goes. Every pull request is checked against real PostgreSQL, Keycloak and Temporal, and a failing test turns it red. Flow API operations No public API operations. /healthz , /readyz and /metrics are served on the admin port, which is not published and is not part of api/openapi.bundled.yaml . The public port answers 404 for all three (negative checks S1.4 and S1.5). How to try it Start the stack and the local forwards (the admin port is reachable only through them): sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh , then sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh up . In Bruno open the collection docs/acceptance/guide/bruno/ , select the local environment and run the folder S1 . Or headless: sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/api-test.sh S1 . Bruno request What it checks S1/S1-1.bru GET /healthz lists every dependency S1/S1-2.bru GET /readyz S1/S1-3.bru GET /metrics S1/S1-4.bru negative: /healthz is not on the public port S1/S1-5.bru negative: /metrics is not on the public port S1/S1-6.bru the Keycloak realm is up with the pinned issuer The step-by-step kit, including stopping Keycloak and PostgreSQL to watch /healthz and /readyz change, is the acceptance guide page for S1 and the kit ; this page does not copy it. Verdict and evidence Verdict: READY, all acceptance criteria pass (QA, 2026-10-25, on main ). Recorded run used: the committed Bruno run acceptance-run.md (2026-10-29, 145 requests and 466 tests, 0 failed) and the kit above. Not re-run for this page. Regression of milestone 1 on main : S1 evidence ."},{"url":"stories/s2.html","title":"S2: A query without the right tenant returns nothing","section":"stories","text":"Story TICK-44. Verdict READY , 2026-10-26, recorded in the acceptance kit . The first run failed criterion 3; it was fixed (TICK-233, TICK-235) and run again. What a user can now do Rely on the database itself to keep each tenant's rows away from every other tenant. A query that names no tenant, an empty tenant, or a tenant left over from an earlier request returns nothing. Rely on each part of the platform (API, worker, relay, purge, onboarding, group sync, backup) logging in as its own database user, each able to do only its own job. Rely on CI to refuse a migration that grants a role more than db/grants.yaml lists. Flow API operations No public API operations. The story is a database and CI guarantee. Its Bruno requests only probe the tenant boundary through the existing GET /api/v1/me and a record id. How to try it sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh (seeds tenants A and B). In Bruno run the folder S2 , or headless sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/api-test.sh S2 . Bruno request What it checks S2/S2-1.bru , S2-2.bru sign in as a tenant A user and a tenant B user S2/S2-3.bru GET /me is tenant A S2/S2-4.bru GET /me is tenant B, none of A's groups S2/S2-5.bru negative: tenant B named in headers, query and body is ignored S2/S2-6.bru negative: the same from tenant B S2/S2-7.bru negative: a tenant B record by id returns nothing The database half (catalogue queries, make isolation ) is in the acceptance guide page for S2 and the kit ; this page does not copy it. Verdict and evidence Verdict: READY, all 4 criteria pass (QA, 2026-10-26). Recorded run used: the committed Bruno run acceptance-run.md (2026-10-29, 145 requests and 466 tests, 0 failed) and the kit. Not re-run for this page. Regression of milestone 1 on main : S2 evidence ."},{"url":"stories/s3.html","title":"S3: A tenant user signs in and is scoped to their tenant only","section":"stories","text":"Story TICK-45. Verdict READY , 2026-10-27, recorded in the acceptance kit (16 of 16 checks pass). What a user can now do Sign in through Keycloak as a seeded user and call GET /api/v1/me . The answer holds the tenant, the user id and the groups. Trust that the tenant comes only from the signed token and the platform's own binding table. A tenant id in a header, the query string or the body changes nothing. Be refused with a token that names no organization or more than one, an ID token, or a token from an issuer the platform does not trust. Flow API operations Operation Method and path Summary Responses getMe GET /api/v1/me The calling user and tenant 200, 401, 429, 500, 501 How to try it sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh . In Bruno run the folder S3 , or headless sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/api-test.sh S3 . Operation Bruno request file getMe S3/S3-2.bru (tenant user), S3/S3-4.bru (tenant admin) getMe negatives S3-5 no token, S3-6 garbage, S3-7 tampered, S3-8 ID token, S3-10 wrong issuer, S3-12 operator token, S3-15 no organization, S3-21 two organizations The sign-in and setup requests are S3-1 , S3-3 , S3-9 , S3-11 , S3-13 to S3-14 and S3-16 to S3-20 . The step-by-step kit is the acceptance guide page for S3 and the kit ; this page does not copy it. Verdict and evidence Verdict: READY, all 3 criteria, story step 3 and the cross-tenant check pass (QA, 2026-10-27). Recorded run used: the committed Bruno run acceptance-run.md (2026-10-29, 145 requests and 466 tests, 0 failed) and the kit's own transcript . Not re-run for this page."},{"url":"stories/s4.html","title":"S4: An operator onboards a tenant and its admin signs in","section":"stories","text":"Story TICK-46. Verdict READY , 2026-10-28, recorded in the acceptance kit . What a user can now do As an operator, create a new tenant with one API call. It creates the tenant's sign-in organization, connects the tenant's own identity provider and invites the tenant's first admin. Send the same call again with the same Idempotency-Key : the same answer, and nothing is created twice. As the invited admin, sign in with the company account and see only your own tenant. Someone who already signs in through another tenant's identity provider cannot be made this tenant's admin. Flow API operations Operation Method and path Summary Responses onboardTenant POST /api/v1/operator/tenants Onboard a tenant (operator token only) 200, 201, 400, 401, 403, 409, 422, 429, 500, 501 The route is served by nava-onboard (operator token only), not by the tenant ingress. How to try it sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh , then sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh up (it forwards the onboarding service to the host). In Bruno run the folder S4 , or headless sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/api-test.sh S4 . Operation Bruno request file onboardTenant S4/S4-8.bru onboard a new tenant; S4/S4-9.bru replay with the same key onboardTenant negatives S4-3 no token, S4-4 a tenant admin's token, S4-5 route not on the tenant ingress, S4-6 no key, S4-7 private identity provider address, S4-10 same key with a different body, S4-11 and S4-12 domain already claimed, S4-13 tenant id in body or header ignored, S4-14 operator routes only S4-1 and S4-2 sign in as the operator and as a tenant admin. The step-by-step kit is the acceptance guide page for S4 and the kit ; this page does not copy it. Verdict and evidence Verdict: READY (QA, 2026-10-28, on the story branch up to date with main ). Recorded run used: the committed Bruno run acceptance-run.md (2026-10-29, 145 requests and 466 tests, 0 failed) and the kit. Not re-run for this page. Regression of milestone 1 on main : S4 evidence ."},{"url":"stories/s5.html","title":"S5: Group changes and deprovisioning take effect within 10 minutes","section":"stories","text":"Story TICK-47. Verdict READY , 2026-10-27, recorded in the acceptance kit . What a user can now do As an administrator, remove someone from a team in Keycloak: the platform stops treating them as a member within 10 minutes, even on a sign-in they already hold. As an administrator, disable someone: they cannot sign in again, and the sign-in they already hold stops working within 10 minutes. Rely on each customer's copy of its users and teams holding only its own people. Rely on a set of automated attacks on sign-in (forged or swapped tokens, tampering with the login page, another customer's login system) being refused, on every PR. Flow API operations Operation Method and path Summary Responses getMe GET /api/v1/me The calling user and tenant 200, 401, 429, 500, 501 How to try it sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh (it also runs one group sync). In Bruno run the folder S5 , or headless sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/api-test.sh S5 . The polling requests wait for the sync, so the folder takes several minutes. Operation Bruno request file getMe before and after a group change S5/S5-4.bru , then the poll S5/S5-6.bru (the same token loses its group), S5/S5-10.bru (restored) getMe after a disable S5/S5-13.bru (the same token gets 401 tenant_unresolved ), S5/S5-17.bru (re-enabled) The Keycloak changes themselves ( S5-5 , S5-8 , S5-11 , S5-15 ) and the sign-in negative ( S5-12 ) call Keycloak, not the platform API; S5-3 , S5-7 and S5-14 are the unaffected control user. The step-by-step kit is the acceptance guide page for S5 and the kit ; this page does not copy it. Verdict and evidence Verdict: READY, all six criteria pass (QA, 2026-10-27, fresh stack). Recorded run used: the committed Bruno run acceptance-run.md (2026-10-29, 145 requests and 466 tests, 0 failed), the kit, and the timed attack-suite report ci5-report.md . Not re-run for this page."},{"url":"stories/s6.html","title":"S6: API ground rules on the first endpoints","section":"stories","text":"Story TICK-48. Verdict READY , 2026-10-29, recorded in the acceptance kit . Criteria 1, 2 and 4 pass on the stack; criterion 3 (pagination) passes in automated tests only, because no endpoint returned pages when the kit ran (S9 later added list endpoints). What a user can now do Read the API contract at /api/v1/docs ; it lists every endpoint and links the OpenAPI 3.1 file. Get every error in one shape: application/problem+json with a code from one list ( api/errors.yaml ). Resend an onboarding request with the same Idempotency-Key and get the same answer; reusing the key for a different request is refused. Be slowed down with 429 when one tenant floods the API, while other tenants are not affected. Flow API operations Operation Method and path Summary Responses getMe GET /api/v1/me The calling user and tenant 200, 401, 429, 500, 501 The documentation routes /api/v1/docs and /api/v1/openapi.yaml are public and are not operations in the bundle. How to try it sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh . In Bruno run the folder S6 , or headless sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/api-test.sh S6 . What Bruno request file contract and docs page S6/S6-1.bru ( /api/v1/openapi.yaml ), S6/S6-2.bru ( /api/v1/docs ) getMe wrong method S6/S6-7.bru (with a token), S6/S6-8.bru (no token) unknown routes S6-4 , S6-5 , S6-6 request id and trace header S6-9 no request input in an error S6-10 rate limit S6-12 tenant A gets 429 while tenant B succeeds, S6-13 served again after Retry-After S6-3 and S6-11 sign in. The step-by-step kit is the acceptance guide page for S6 and the kit ; this page does not copy it. Verdict and evidence Verdict: READY (QA, 2026-10-29, on main , fresh stack). Recorded run used: the committed Bruno run acceptance-run.md (2026-10-29, 145 requests and 466 tests, 0 failed) and the kit's own transcript . Not re-run for this page."},{"url":"stories/s7.html","title":"S7: A committed outbox row runs exactly one tenant-scoped workflow","section":"stories","text":"Story TICK-49. Verdict READY , 2026-10-27, recorded in the acceptance kit . All 5 criteria and the cross-tenant check pass. What a user can now do Rely on background work starting exactly once when the platform saves a change that should start it, even if the hand-off is repeated or the process doing it crashes halfway. Rely on follow-up messages for that work arriving in the order they were saved. Rely on one tenant's background work being unable to touch another tenant's work: the attempt is refused and raises an alert. As an operator, watch all of this in the Temporal UI. Flow API operations No public API operations. The work is started by the relay from the outbox, not through api/openapi.bundled.yaml . How to try it Start the stack with the operator profile so the Temporal UI runs, then sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh and sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh up (it forwards the Temporal UI). Write the demo rows: make demo-outbox DEDUPE_KEY=try-it , and once more with the same key. In Bruno run the folder S7 , or headless sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/api-test.sh S7 . What Bruno request file the demo's workflow has exactly one execution S7/S7-1.bru its history holds the start and two signals in commit order S7/S7-2.bru The step-by-step kit, including the crash case, is the acceptance guide page for S7 and the kit ; this page does not copy it. Verdict and evidence Verdict: READY (QA, 2026-10-27, fresh stack on the story branch with main merged). Recorded run used: the committed Bruno run acceptance-run.md (2026-10-29, 145 requests and 466 tests, 0 failed) and the kit. Not re-run for this page."},{"url":"stories/s8.html","title":"S8: A tenant admin publishes an incident type and reads every version","section":"stories","text":"Story TICK-333 (kit TICK-346). Verdict READY , 2026-11-01, recorded in the acceptance kit . All five criteria pass. What a user can now do Start every new tenant with three ticket types, incident , request and change , at version 1. As a tenant admin, change the incident type and publish it as version 2. Every version reads back exactly as it was published. Be refused when a change would break existing tickets (a field changing type): 422 schema_breaking_change , and nothing is stored. As another user of the tenant, read the types but not publish them ( 403 ). Another tenant sees only its own types. Flow API operations Operation Method and path Summary Responses listEntityTypes GET /api/v1/entity-types List the tenant's entity types 200, 400, 401, 429, 500, 501 getEntityType GET /api/v1/entity-types/{key} The current version of an entity type 200, 401, 404, 429, 500, 501 publishEntityTypeVersion POST /api/v1/entity-types/{key}/versions Publish the next version of an entity type (tenant admin) 201, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 422, 429, 500, 501 getEntityTypeVersion GET /api/v1/entity-types/{key}/versions/{n} One published version of an entity type 200, 400, 401, 404, 429, 500, 501 onboardTenant POST /api/v1/operator/tenants Onboard a tenant (operator token only) 200, 201, 400, 401, 403, 409, 422, 429, 500, 501 How to try it sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/seed.sh (run it again if you seeded before this story: it adds the admins group and the three types), then sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh up . In Bruno run the folder S8 , or headless sh docs/acceptance/guide/tools/api-test.sh S8 . The kit also names the stack case deploy/compose/test/cases/s8_entity_types.sh for the database half of AC5. Operation Bruno request file listEntityTypes S8/S8-4.bru (tenant A user), S8/S8-14.bru (tenant B user) getEntityType S8/S8-5.bru , S8/S8-10.bru (current version unchanged), S8/S8-15.bru (tenant B's own) publishEntityTypeVersion S8/S8-11.bru publish (201), S8/S8-8.bru breaking change (422), S8/S8-7.bru non-admin (403) getEntityTypeVersion S8/S8-6.bru and S8/S8-13.bru (version 1), S8/S8-12.bru (the new version), S8/S8-9.bru (nothing stored), S8/S8-16.bru and S8/S8-17.bru (the same 404) onboardTenant S8/S8-19.bru (a new tenant starts with three types) S8-1 to S8-3 and S8-18 sign in. The step-by-step kit is the acceptance guide page for S8 and the kit ; this page does not copy it. Verdict and evidence Verdict: READY, five of five criteria pass, plus the cross-tenant check (QA). Recorded run used: the kit's committed result table. Its fresh-stack run executed in GitHub Actions; that run is not linked here, because handbook evidence points to committed files only. The committed Bruno run acceptance-run.md (2026-10-29) predates S8 and does not cover it. Not re-run for this page."},{"url":"system/core-acl.html","title":"Core: record ACL (ParsePolicy, PolicyStore.Publish, CanRead/CanWrite, explain)","section":"system","text":"Purpose Each entity type has a versioned, allow-only access policy. It is checked in four steps: an admin's JSON is parsed and checked, the policy is published as a new acl_versions row, every read and write is evaluated against the newest version, and the explainer reports a decision with the same evaluator. The policy model and every rule are in the normative record-acl.md ; field masks are on core-masks . Entry point HTTP: getEntityTypeAccess , putEntityTypeAccess , explainAccess in internal/server/access.go (tenant admins only, authz.RequireTenantAdminTx first). Evaluation: authz.ACL ( internal/authz/aclstore.go ), the authz.Authorizer the pipeline and the record handlers hold. Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Parse and check the PUT body: database shape rules first (so no raw constraint error), then unique rule ids and mask rules; collect every referenced id authz.ParsePolicy ( policycheck.go ) A stored policy always decodes TestParsePolicyAcceptsTheShapesTheCheckAccepts , TestParsePolicyRefusesWithPointers , TestParsePolicyCollectsEveryIdAsATenantRef 1b Mask fields must be top-level properties of the current schema; every id must be a live group or user of the transaction's tenant authz.CheckMaskFields , authz.NotInTenant ( explain.go ) I1: a policy names only its own tenant's ids; foreign and unknown ids answer alike TestCheckMaskFields 2 Publish version N+1 under the entity type's row lock, with one config / publish audit entry in the same transaction PolicyStore.Publish , publishLocked ( publish.go ); PolicyStore.PublishIfNone for the onboarding default audit_commit_check refuses an unaudited insert TestP335ACLPublishWithoutAnAuditEntryIsRefused 3 Load the newest policy of the type in the caller's transaction; refuse a principal of another tenant first PolicyStore.Current , PolicyStore.QueuePolicy , ACL.policy No published version fails closed TestPolicyStoreReadsTheNewestVersionOfItsTenant , TestNoPublishedACLRefusesUserWrites 3a Read one record: ACL.CanRead -\u003e Allows(pol, p, read, row) ; shares loaded pair-matched by SharesFor aclstore.go , acl.go Unreadable is the same 404 as missing TestACLCanRead , TestSharesMatchOnKindAndID 3b Write: ACL.CanWrite(before, after) ; both rows must be in write scope aclstore.go A write cannot move a record out of scope TestACLCanWrite , TestWriteScopeIsEnforcedBeforeAndAfter 3c List: CompileRead / ReadBranches render the same planned rules as SQL inside the query acl.go I5: ACL inside the query, Go and SQL agree TestCompileReadAgreesWithAllows , TestI5Agreement , TestI5SharedWithIsPairMatched 4 Explain: the target user's decision with the same Allows , and only the masked fields the asking admin may read authz.Explain , authz.PrincipalOf ( explain.go ) The explainer cannot disagree with enforcement TestExplainMatchesTheEnforcementOnState , TestExplainNeverListsAFieldTheAdminCannotRead , TestExplainWithNoPolicyDeniesAtVersionZero Errors Error HTTP not a tenant admin ( RequireTenantAdminTx ) 403 forbidden unknown or foreign entity type, no published version (GET), unknown record or user (explain) 404 not_found (one body) If-Match missing or * 428 if_match_required *authz.ConflictError (stale If-Match ) 412 version_conflict with the current ETag body not a JSON object 400 validation_failed ParsePolicy / CheckMaskFields / NotInTenant problems 422 invalid_access_policy with pointers authz.ErrNotVisible on a record read 404 not_found authz.ErrForbidden on a write 403 forbidden Notes The order of the PUT checks (admin, entity type, If-Match , body, write) and the default incident ACL are defined in the normative note. There is no separate \"compiler\" stage: CompileRead is the SQL rendering of the same planned rules Allows evaluates. Links Tables acl_versions , record_shares : database section . Masks: core-masks . Where the pipeline calls the ACL: core-write-pipeline . Isolation and ACL guarantees: security section ."},{"url":"system/core-cascades.html","title":"Core: cascade bounds (I9)","section":"system","text":"Purpose A record write can start a workflow, which can write a record, which can start another workflow. ADR-001 I9 bounds that chain two ways: every write carries a causation (origin id and depth) and a write deeper than the limit is refused, and a workflow definition starts at most once per (tenant, origin, record). This page shows where both checks run inside the write pipeline. Entry point Depth: Pipeline.perform in internal/records/pipeline/pipeline.go compares Command.Causation.Depth with config.CascadeDepthLimit ( internal/platform/config/config.go ) before any statement but the tenant read. Start key: cascade.ClaimStart(ctx, tx, cascade.Key) in internal/records/cascade/cascade.go , called by the event sink in the triggering write's transaction; a repeat is audited by pipeline.AuditSuppressed . Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Refuse a given origin at depth 0 and a workflow write at depth 0; mint the origin for a depth-0 write Command.check , Pipeline.perform Origin and depth never come from a request or payload (A5-3, A5-7) TestDepthZeroWritesMintTheirOrigin , TestWorkflowWriteAtDepthZeroIsInvalidEntry 2 Refuse a write beyond the depth limit; write one cascade_too_deep audit entry in a transaction of its own on the TxRunner , committed before Write returns Pipeline.rejectTooDeep , appendTooDeep ( audit.go ) I9 depth bound; nothing in the caller's transaction changes TestCI10_DepthBeyondLimitRejectedAndAuditedOnTheRunner , TestCI10_TooDeepUpdateOfAnotherTenantsRecordLeaksNothing , TestTooDeepAuditFailure 3 Store the causation on the record row with the write write.causation The start key is read from the row, never passed in TestClaimStartReadsTheKeyFromTheRecord 4 Claim the start key K(tenant, origin, record, definition): lock the record row (naming the tenant), insert into cascade_start cascade.ClaimStart A5: at most one start per key; per tenant TestCI10_StartKeyIsPerTenant , TestCI10_ConcurrentClaimsOfOneKey , TestCI10_RetriedActivityCreatesNoSecondStart 5 Claimed: enqueue the start through outbox.EnqueueStart , which only takes a claimed cascade.Claim outbox producer I6: starts only through the outbox TestCascadeStartGuards 6 Repeat: enqueue nothing, append a cascade_cycle entry, let the write commit pipeline.AuditSuppressed A5-4: a cycle is suppressed, not failed TestCIA17CycleEntries , TestCI10_WriteBackToTriggeringRecordCommits , TestCI10_SiblingAndSelfRetriggeringWritesAreNotCycles Errors Error Meaning outbox.ErrCascadeTooDeep depth past config.CascadeDepthLimit ; joined with the audit failure if the entry could not be written cascade.ErrRecordNotFound no such record in the transaction's tenant, or no tenant cascade.ErrNoOrigin the record row has no causation cascade.ErrInvalidKey no record or definition in the key; AuditSuppressed called with a claimed start None of these reach an HTTP client: the record routes always write at depth 0. Notes The limit's value lives only in internal/platform/config/config.go ; this page does not copy it. Links The workflow side (outbox, relay, interpreter): workflows section . Tables cascade_start , outbox , audit_log : database section . The write this hangs off: core-write-pipeline ."},{"url":"system/core-masks.html","title":"Core: field masks on read and write","section":"system","text":"Purpose A field mask hides one top-level property from every principal its read subject does not name, and makes it unwritable for every principal its write subject does not name. Masks live in the acl_versions document of the entity type, in its masks array beside rules , and are published, versioned and audited with the rules ( core-acl ). The rules are in the normative record-field-masks.md . Entry point Write: write.canWriteFields in internal/records/pipeline/pipeline.go calls ACL.CanWriteFields ( internal/authz/aclstore.go ), which calls authz.CheckWriteFields ( internal/authz/mask.go ). Read: the record handlers call authz.Shape through shapeRecord ( internal/server/records.go ); lists call authz.CheckQueryFields ( internal/server/records_list.go ); the audit timeline gets the caller's masks from ACL.ReadMasks . Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Decide per field and principal: Readable / Writable ; only Groups and Users in a mask subject match; every mask on a field must admit authz.Readable , authz.Writable ( mask.go ) A2: one answer per caller and field, row-independent TestReadableAndWritableFollowGroupsAndUsers , TestRowDependentMaskSubjectsNeverMatch , TestEveryMaskOnAFieldMustAdmit 2 Write check, first on the caller's data (before defaults, validation, state, CanWrite , unique); system sources skip it write.canWriteFields , ACL.CanWriteFields , authz.CheckWriteFields , authz.WrittenFields I5: masks apply on write; no other error depends on a masked field TestFieldCheckRunsBeforeValidationAndTheStateModel , TestFieldCheckSeesOnlyTheCallersWrite , TestFieldNotWritableWritesNothing 2a A patch key the caller cannot read counts as a write whatever its value authz.CheckWriteFields A patch is no oracle for a masked value TestUnreadablePatchKeyIsAlwaysAWrite , TestPatchingAMaskedFieldIsRefusedEvenWithItsStoredValue 3 Shape every record body and every history before / after authz.Shape , authz.MaskedFields I5: masks apply on read; input never mutated TestShapeDropsMaskedFieldsAndNeverMutates 4 Refuse a list filter or sort on a field the caller cannot read, before any SQL authz.CheckQueryFields A hidden field cannot be filtered, sorted or counted TestCheckQueryFields 5 Redact masked fields in audit-timeline diffs ACL.ReadMasks -\u003e read.FieldMasks The reader role needs no new grant see internal/audit/read tests Errors Error HTTP *authz.ErrFieldNotWritable{Fields} 422 field_not_writable , one pointer per field, rule mask *authz.ErrMaskedField{Field} 400 masked_field a mask naming requester , assignee or shared , or an unknown property 422 invalid_access_policy at publish Links Storage ( acl_versions.masks ): database section . Masking guarantees: security section ."},{"url":"system/core-metadata.html","title":"Core: metadata engine (entity definitions and versions)","section":"system","text":"Purpose A tenant describes each entity type (incident, request, change, or its own) as a JSON Schema 2020-12 document plus a number prefix, fixed-column mapping and state model. The metadata engine turns that definition into a *metadata.Compiled , the one object every record write validates against, and decides whether a new version can be published over the current one. This page shows where each step lives; the rules themselves are in the normative metadata-engine.md . Entry point HTTP: createEntityType and publishEntityTypeVersion in internal/server/entitytypes.go . Go: entitytypes.Store.Create and entitytypes.Store.Publish ( internal/entitytypes/entitytypes.go ), which call metadata.Compile and metadata.Classify . Read side, used by the record pipeline: entitytypes.Store.Current and entitytypes.Store.Version (the pipeline.DefinitionReader interface), and entitytypes.Store.QueueCurrent for the batched prefetch. internal/metadata is pure: no database, network or file-system import. internal/entitytypes is the database half. Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Refuse a schema that marks a property secret and also requires it, or carries a secret-looking default entitytypes.CheckSchemaSecrets ( audit.go ), entitytypes.CheckSchemaSecretRequired ( secretrequired.go ) ADR-004-E5 D9 TestCheckSchemaSecretRequired , TestAdversarialSecretDefaultIsRefused 2 Scan size and depth, walk the schema (local $ref only, closed root), compile with a fresh jsonschema compiler and a deny-all loader metadata.Compile ( compile.go ), denyAllLoader , walker.walk , walker.checkRefs One compiler per call, so no tenant sees another's schema TestCompileRefusesExternalRefs , TestCompilerLoaderDeniesEverything , TestSharedCompilerWouldLeak , TestCompileCapsAreInclusive 3 Check defaults against their own schema, fixed-column types and the state enum metadata.Compile A definition the pipeline cannot satisfy never stores TestCompileRefusesBadStateModelsAndShapes , TestCompileAcceptsBaseDefinition 4 Create: key, then prefix uniqueness; insert type and version 1 with compat = 'initial' ; append the config audit entry entitytypes.Store.Create , auditVersion Every config change is audited in the same transaction TestCreateRefusals 5 Publish: lock the type row, compile the next version, classify it against the current one entitytypes.Store.Publish , metadata.Classify ( classify.go ) Fail-closed classification: anything not listed is Breaking TestClassify , TestClassifyRemoveTransition , TestPublishRefusalsAndFailures 6 Insert version N+1 (number from max(version) + 1 under the row lock), move current_version , audit entitytypes.Store.Publish Concurrent publishes queue and number with no gap or duplicate TestPublishRefusalsAndFailures 7 Change numbering mode ( gaps or gapless ) with its own audited config entry entitytypes.Store.SetNumberingMode See core-numbering TestP336fSetNumberingMode , TestP336fNumberingModeCrossTenant 8 Use at write time: defaults then validation against the current version; stored rows are read under the version they were written with metadata.Compiled.ApplyDefaults , metadata.Compiled.Validate ( validate.go ), called from pipeline.write.create / write.update I4: JSON Schema runs before every write TestApplyDefaults , TestValidateReportsEachKindOfFailure , TestUpdateUnderNewVersionFillsDefaults The tenant on every statement comes from the caller's tenancy.InTenantTx transaction (row-level security plus the explicit tenant_id the inserts take). See core-tenancy . Errors Error Where HTTP *metadata.ErrInvalidDefinition (sorted JSON pointers) metadata.Compile 422 invalid_definition entitytypes.ErrKeyExists Store.Create 409 entity_type_exists entitytypes.ErrPrefixTaken Store.Create 409 number_prefix_taken *entitytypes.BreakingError{Paths} Store.Publish 422 schema_breaking_change *entitytypes.SecretRequiredError CheckSchemaSecretRequired 422 definition_secret_required *entitytypes.SecretInContentError CheckSchemaSecrets 422 config_secret_in_content entitytypes.ErrNotFound Store.Publish , Store.GetVersion 404 not_found (another tenant's type is the same 404) The mapping is in internal/server/entitytypes.go ; codes and statuses are in api/errors.yaml . Notes Seed definitions incident , request and change live in internal/metadata/templates/ ( metadata.Template ) and are seeded at onboarding by entitytypes.Store.Seed . The compatibility classes and the caps are listed only in the normative note; this page does not repeat them. Links Tables: database section ( entity_definitions , entity_definition_versions ). Next step of a write: core-write-pipeline . ADR-001 I4 and I8 (configuration, not code): ADR summaries ."},{"url":"system/core-numbering.html","title":"Core: record numbering (gaps and gapless)","section":"system","text":"Purpose Every record gets a human number, \u003cprefix\u003e\u003c7 digits\u003e ( INC0000001 ), unique and increasing per tenant and entity type. Numbers come from one record_counters upsert; there is no sequence (ADR-001 I8). A type's numbering_mode picks where the upsert runs: gaps (default, ADR-005 L2s): in a transaction of its own on the allocator pool, committed before the record is inserted. The counter row is locked for one round trip. A create that fails after its number leaves a **gap by design**. gapless (ADR-005 L1): last in the create's own write batch, so a rollback gives the number back, at the cost of holding the counter lock until the create commits. The decision and its rules are in the normative ADR-005 and the numbering section of record-write-pipeline.md . Entry point write.create in internal/records/pipeline/pipeline.go reads Compiled.NumberingMode() and calls write.allocate ( alloc.go ) for a gaps type. PoolRunner.Allocate -\u003e tenancy.AllocTx -\u003e store.QueueAllocate ( internal/records/store/numbering.go ). gapless : store.QueueInsertNumbered in write.writeCreate ( batch.go ). Mode change: entitytypes.Store.SetNumberingMode . Pool wiring: cmd/nava-core/allocator.go ( NAVA_ALLOCATOR_DATABASE_URL ). Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Take the number only after the replay check, validation, the write check and the unique and group checks write.create I4: a refused, invalid or replayed create never moves the counter TestRefusedCreatesLeaveTheCounter , TestCreateReplayTakesNoNumber 2 gaps : allocate in its own transaction on the allocator pool; tenant from the create's transaction, never a parameter write.allocate , PoolRunner.Allocate , tenancy.AllocTx , store.QueueAllocate ADR-005 A1, A2, A4 TestCreateTakesItsNumberInATransactionOfItsOwn , TestAllocationTransactionIsTenantScoped 3 Check the allocation names this create's tenant, type, scope '' and epoch 0; store its now() as created_at write.allocate ADR-005 A2, A7 TestCreateChecksTheAllocation , TestAllocationIsTheCreatesAndDatesTheRecord 4 Give up past the allocation timeout with ErrRetryable ; record acquire and total time PoolRunner.Allocate , AllocMetrics ADR-005 A5, A8 TestAllocationTimeoutIsRetryableAndMeasured 5 The allocator pool is separate from the API pool and logs in as nava_app only tenancy.AllocPoolConfig , tenancy.CheckAllocatorRole ADR-005 A3; I3 TestAllocatorPoolDoesNotStarveThePool , TestAllocatorPoolRefusesABypassRLSRole 6 A failure after the allocation commits leaves a gap; numbers are never reused gaps_integration_test.go Gaps by design TestRolledBackCreateLeavesAGap , TestCreateRollsBackAtEveryStage 7 gapless : upsert last in the write batch; rollback returns the number store.QueueInsertNumbered ADR-005 L1 TestRolledBackCreateNumberIsReused , TestConcurrentCreatesNumberWithoutGapsThroughPgBouncer 8 A mode change applies to creates that start after it; both modes upsert the same row entitytypes.Store.SetNumberingMode No number used twice TestModeSwitchNeverReusesANumber , TestNumberingModesDoNotInterfere 9 Format \u003cprefix\u003e\u003c7 digits\u003e , growing past 7; lists sort on created_at store.FormatNumber Unique per tenant and type TestFormatNumber , TestNumbersPerTenantAndType Errors Error HTTP ErrRetryable (allocation past its timeout) 503 retry_later , nothing committed but possibly the number (a gap) allocation not this create's (tenant, type, scope, epoch) 500 internal_error (a bug, never expected) entitytypes.ErrInvalidNumberingMode refused before anything is written Notes Metrics: nava_numbering_alloc_acquire_seconds , nava_numbering_alloc_seconds , nava_numbering_alloc_timeouts . Measured create latency under both modes: benchmarks page . Links Table record_counters : database section . The allocator transaction: core-tenancy . The surrounding write: core-write-pipeline ."},{"url":"system/core-tenancy.html","title":"Core: tenant transactions (InTenantTx, batching, allocator pool)","section":"system","text":"Purpose Every tenant-scoped database access in nava-core runs inside tenancy.InTenantTx . It is the only Go code that sets app.tenant_id , the setting every row-level security policy reads, and it sets it in the same round trip as BEGIN . This page walks the path from a verified token to a scoped transaction, and the helpers the record pipeline uses on top of it (first-batch BEGIN, commit batch, memo, allocator transaction, lock timeout). The rules are in the normative tenant-transactions.md , tenant-tx-batching.md and tenant-resolution.md . Entry point Resolution: internal/platform/auth middleware calls identity.IdentityResolver.Resolve ; handlers read the tenant with identity.FromContext . Never from a path, query, body or header. tenancy.InTenantTx(ctx, pool, tenantID, fn, opts...) in internal/tenancy/tenancy.go . tenancy.TenantOf(ctx, tx) : the tenant a transaction runs under, used by the pipeline, the ACL store and the cascade claim instead of a parameter. Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Resolve the tenant from the validated token and the tenant binding; only an active tenant resolves internal/platform/auth , keycloak.Adapter.Resolve , pgstore.Binding I2: tenant only from a platform-verified source TestNoTenant (empty tenant), resolution tests listed in tenant-resolution.md 2 Open the transaction: BEGIN; SELECT set_config('app.tenant_id', \u003cuuid\u003e, true) in one simple-query round trip, transaction-local tenancy.InTenantTx , tenancy.BeginTenant I1: RLS sees the tenant before any statement of fn TestBeginSetsTheTenantInOneRoundTrip , TestTenantIsTransactionLocal , TestTenantNotVisibleThroughPgBouncer 3 With BeginInFirstBatch() , defer BEGIN into the first pgx.Batch (BEGIN and tenant first, then the caller's statements) lazyTx.SendBatch , lazyTx.begin ( firstbatch.go ), tenancy.FirstBatchTenant No statement runs before the tenant; a failure aborts everything TestFirstBatchRunsUnderTheTenant , TestFirstBatchCannotReachAnotherTenant , TestFirstBatchFailureKeepsNothing 4 Per-transaction memo for prefetched reads (definition, policy, the row a create stored); savepoint rollback undoes it tenancy.Remember , Recall , Forget ( scopedTx , memo ) The memo dies with the transaction, which has one tenant TestMemoIsPerTransaction , TestSavepointRollbackUndoesItsMemo 5 Queue statements for the round trip that commits tenancy.ExecAtCommit , tenancy.QueueAtCommit , scopedTx.commitBatch A failing queued statement skips COMMIT TestExecAtCommit , TestCreateRecordFailureInFirstOrLastTripLeavesNothing 6 Bound one statement's lock wait (the idempotency claim) without a round trip tenancy.QueueUnderLockTimeout ( locktimeout.go ) Only that statement waits at most the bound (55P03) TestQueueUnderLockTimeout , TestQueueUnderLockTimeoutFloor 7 Run a number allocation in its own transaction on the allocator pool, tenant from the create's transaction, one pipelined send, asynchronous commit tenancy.AllocTx , tenancy.AllocPoolConfig , tenancy.CheckAllocatorRole ( alloc.go ) I3: the pool logs in as nava_app only, never SUPERUSER or BYPASSRLS TestAllocTxIsOneSendAndLeavesNoSetting , TestAllocTxFailureLeaksNothing , TestAllocatorPoolRefusesOtherRoles 8 End: COMMIT on success; ROLLBACK on error (error returned unwrapped, a rollback failure joined) or panic (re-raised) tenancy.InTenantTx The setting ends with the transaction TestErrorRollsBack , TestFailedRollbackIsJoined , TestPanicRollsBackAndReraises , TestCommitFailureIsReturned Every request in the tenancy, pipeline and server tests runs through tenancytest.Guard , a pgx tracer that records any statement sent on a connection with no tenant. The cross-tenant path registry is internal/tenancy/isolation . Record-create round trips (TICK-692) A gaps record create is four round trips: Round trip What 1 BEGIN + tenant, idempotency claim under QueueUnderLockTimeout , prefetch (definition, ACL policy) 2 checks: unique locks and lookups, assignment group, payload preview 3 number: tenancy.AllocTx on the allocator pool (its own COMMIT, asynchronous) 4 INSERT records (asserted), write key, audit entry, idempotency completion, COMMIT The statement-level table and the history of the budget are in tenant-tx-batching.md ; measured latency against the budget is on the benchmarks page . Errors Error Meaning tenancy.ErrNoTenant uuid.Nil tenant, or TenantOf on a transaction with no tenant; no transaction is opened tenancy.ErrAllocatorRole the allocator pool would log in as another role or a privileged one; startup refuses SQLSTATE 55P03 a statement under QueueUnderLockTimeout waited past its bound; the HTTP layer answers 409 idempotency_in_flight for the claim Notes navalint setconfig reports set_config / app.tenant_id outside internal/tenancy ; navalint tenancyconn reports .Conn() and .LargeObjects() on a pgx.Tx outside it. The database grants are the control; the lint is defence in depth. The tracer exports no SQL text and no parameter values ( TestTracedTxExportsNoValues ). Links Policies, roles and grants: database section . Who uses it next: core-write-pipeline , core-numbering ."},{"url":"system/core-write-pipeline.html","title":"Core: record write pipeline (write, read, list)","section":"system","text":"Purpose pipeline.Write is the only way to create or update a record (ADR-001 I4). It runs inside the caller's tenancy.InTenantTx transaction and never commits, so the record, its write key, its audit entry and whatever the EventSink wrote commit together or not at all. Reads go through pipeline.Get and pipeline.List ; only the pipeline imports internal/records/store . The rules are in the normative record-write-pipeline.md . Entry point HTTP: createRecord ( handlers.CreateRecord , internal/server/records.go ), patchRecord ( internal/server/records_patch.go ), getRecord , listRecords ( internal/server/records_list.go ). Go: (*pipeline.Pipeline).Write(ctx, tx, pipeline.Command) in internal/records/pipeline/pipeline.go ; pipeline.Get , pipeline.QueueGet and pipeline.List in internal/records/pipeline/read.go . Other writers (workflows, rules) call the same Write with a system Source ; the architecture test TestRecordWritersOnlyInPipeline fails on any other writer of records . Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Check the command's shape: op and record, source, a user write with an idempotency key, a depth-0 write that names an origin ( Write mints it), a workflow write at depth 0, an AI write without source_ref Command.check A cascade origin is minted, never given (ADR-001 A5) TestWriteRefusesBadCommands , TestDepthZeroWritesMintTheirOrigin 2 Tenant from the transaction; an actor of another tenant is refused Pipeline.perform , tenancy.TenantOf I1, I2 TestWriteTakesTheTenantFromTheTransaction , TestWriteRefusesAnActorOfAnotherTenant , TestCrossTenantWritesRefused 3 Prefetch in one batch (definition id, write key, current definition, ACL policy); on the HTTP create the same batch carries BEGIN and the request's idempotency claim Pipeline.QueuePrefetch , Pipeline.prefetch ( batch.go ), idempotency.ResolveClaim in the handler The claim is the transaction's first write TestCreateRecordFailureInFirstOrLastTripLeavesNothing 4 Replay a keyed system write from record_write_keys , per principal replay , store.QueueWriteKeyByKey I7: a retry never acts twice TestIdempotencyKeyReplays , TestIdempotencyKeyIsScopedByPrincipal , TestIdempotencyKeyMismatchConflicts 5 Update only: load the record, check read access (unreadable = not found), then the version write.update , store.Get , authz.SharesFor , ACL.CanRead No version or existence oracle TestUpdateOfAnUnreadableRecordIsNotFound , TestConcurrentUpdatesOneWins 6 Mask write check on the caller's own data, before anything else can depend on a masked field write.canWriteFields -\u003e ACL.CanWriteFields I5 (see core-masks ) TestFieldCheckRunsBeforeValidationAndTheStateModel , TestFieldNotWritableWritesNothing 7 JSON Merge Patch (update), defaults, JSON Schema, state model mergePatch ( patch.go ), Compiled.ApplyDefaults , Compiled.Validate , Compiled.AllowsTransition I4: validation before every write TestCreateValidatesBeforeWriting , TestInvalidCreateWritesNothing , TestIncidentTransitions 8 Split fixed columns out of data ; default requester_id to the caller on a user/AI create store.Record.Split , write.defaultRequester A fixed property is stored only in its column TestFixedColumnsOnlyInTheirColumn 9 Row write check on before and after rows (skipped for rule , workflow , function , system ) write.canWrite -\u003e ACL.CanWrite I5 (see core-acl ) TestWriteChecksTheRowBeforeAndAfter , TestOnlyTheSystemSourcesSkipTheAuthorizer 10 Unique properties: pg_advisory_xact_lock on the canonical value, then a SELECT ; assignment group must be live write.checkUnique , store.LockUnique , store.UniqueTaken , write.checkGroup , store.GroupLive ; batched on create by write.checkCreate Safe under PgBouncer transaction pooling TestUniqueConcurrentCreatesOneWins , TestUniqueConcurrentUpdateAndCreateOneWins , TestCreateChecksEveryUniquePropertyInOneBatch , TestGroupLive 11 Number (create): see core-numbering write.allocate I4: a refused create never moves the counter TestRefusedCreatesLeaveTheCounter 12 Write: create INSERT (queued at commit and asserted against the 201, or sent now); update UPDATE ... version = version + 1 WHERE version = expected write.writeCreateAtCommit , store.QueueInsertRecordAsExpected , write.writeCreate , store.Update One version bump per write (optimistic concurrency) TestInsertRecordAsExpected , TestEveryWriteBumpsVersionOnce , TestCreateChecksWhatTheInsertStored 13 Keyed write row, audit entry (diff built from the stored jsonb; secrets refused), event sink, all in the same transaction write.finish , store.InsertWriteKey , appendRecordEntry -\u003e audit.Append , EventSink.RecordChanged I6 entry point: the sink enqueues outbox rows in the write's transaction TestAuditAndSinkInTheWriteTransaction , TestDiffIsBuiltFromStoredJSONB , TestSecretInRecordDiffIsRefused , TestCreateRollsBackAtEveryStage , TestPipelineKill Read and list Get : pipeline.Get answers a record created in the same transaction from the memo, otherwise store.Get . The handler then calls ACL.CanRead (unreadable is the same 404 as missing) and shapes the body with authz.Shape . pipeline.QueueGet puts the read in the GET's single batch. List : the handler builds the predicate with authz.CompileRead (or authz.ReadBranches for one index-ordered branch per subject) and checks every filter and sort field with authz.CheckQueryFields , before any SQL. store.List ( list.go ) counts (capped at TotalCap ) and pages by keyset over a listable column; a data-property filter runs under DataFilterTimeout and maps a cancel to ErrStatementTimeout . Proving tests: TestCompileReadAgreesWithAllows , TestBranchedStatements , TestTimedOutMapsOnlyADataFilterCancel . Errors Written by hand from the mapping in handlers.recordError ( internal/server/records.go ) and the PATCH handler ( internal/server/records_patch.go ), statuses from api/errors.yaml . Every pipeline error raised after the definition was read is wrapped in *pipeline.ErrAtVersion , so error pointers are built from the version the write used. Pipeline result HTTP code ErrNotFound (also an unreadable record on update) 404 not_found authz.ErrForbidden 403 forbidden *authz.ErrFieldNotWritable 422 field_not_writable *ErrValidation (incl. rule audit_secret_in_diff ) 422 record_invalid *ErrIllegalTransition 422 illegal_transition ErrAssignmentGroupRemoved 422 assignment_group_removed *ErrUniqueViolation 409 unique_violation (names the property only) ErrVersionConflict 412 version_conflict (current ETag re-read) ErrRetryable (deadlock, serialization failure, allocation timeout) 503 retry_later foreign-key 23503 on a fixed column 422 record_invalid (rule reference ) ErrInvalidCommand , *ErrIdempotencyConflict , outbox.ErrCascadeTooDeep 500 internal_error : the HTTP handlers never send a key or causation, so these are system-writer results only The HTTP request's own Idempotency-Key is a separate layer ( idempotency_keys , 409 idempotency_key_reuse / idempotency_in_flight ), not record_write_keys . Notes The handbook generator has no error-table block (the TICK-720 gate was cancelled), so the table above is written by hand from the two files named; check it against them when either changes. The round-trip layout of a create is on core-tenancy . Links Tables: database section . What the event sink starts: workflows section ; cascade bounds on core-cascades ."},{"url":"system/index.html","title":"System map","section":"system","text":"Every container that docker compose up starts from compose.yaml on main , the network each one sits on, and who talks to whom. Each component has its own page (listed below) with its listeners, database role and the work that introduced it. How the code inside a service is organised is not covered here. The map The SVG embeds its Excalidraw scene. To change it, open the file in Excalidraw, edit it, and export again with Embed scene . If the SVG and compose.yaml disagree, the Mermaid version below was updated with the code and is the one to trust. Not drawn, to keep the map readable: every nava-* service exports OTLP to otel-collector:4318 , and Prometheus scrapes the :9090 admin listener of all six nava-* services ( deploy/compose/prometheus/prometheus.yml ). Networks Network Kind Who is on it default publishes ports to the host (loopback only) nava-core, keycloak internal internal: true , no egress every backing service and every nava-* service operator not internal, so the UIs can publish on 127.0.0.1 temporal-ui, temporal (gateway), openobserve, prometheus temporal-backplane internal: true postgres, temporal-schema, temporal-server, temporal (gateway) No platform process joins temporal-backplane , so the server's internode and membership ports have no route from internal . db-roles has no network ( network_mode: none ); it reaches PostgreSQL only over the Unix socket on the pgsocket volume, which only postgres and db-roles mount. deploy/compose/compose.internal.yaml makes default and operator internal as well. Rules every edge follows Every nava-* service reaches PostgreSQL only through PgBouncer. The direct connections are migrate (as nava_migrator , which PgBouncer refuses), db-roles (the superuser, over the socket), Temporal's persistence ( temporal-schema , temporal-server ) and Keycloak's own database. Clients reach Temporal only through the temporal gateway at temporal:7233 , which forwards to temporal-server . The workflow edges are nava-relay (start and signal) and nava-worker (poll nava-tq ). nava-core dials the gateway only for its readiness probe. nava-onboard , nava-groupsync and nava-purge have no Temporal edge. Among the nava-* services, only nava-core publishes a port ( 127.0.0.1:18080 ). No :9090 admin listener is published. The other published ports are Keycloak ( 127.0.0.1:18081 ) and, with the operator profile, OpenObserve ( 127.0.0.1:5080 ) and Prometheus ( 127.0.0.1:9091 ), all on loopback. Everything else is reached from the host through docs/acceptance/guide/tools/tunnels.sh (local forwards on 127.0.0.1) or docker compose exec . Components Component Kind Listeners (container) Host port DB role(s) Introduced by postgres backing :5432 , Unix socket none (server) PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) pgbouncer backing :5432 none pgbouncer_auth (lookup) PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) nava-db: db-roles, migrate one-shot none none superuser; nava_migrator PLAN-001 T03 keycloak backing :8080 , :9000 management 127.0.0.1:18081 keycloak PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) temporal-schema one-shot none none temporal PLAN-001 T28 temporal-server backing :7233 frontend (+ internode) none temporal PLAN-001 T28 temporal (gateway) backing :7233 none none PLAN-001 T02, gateway since T28 temporal-ui operator profile :8080 none (tunnel 18233) none PLAN-001 T28 otel-collector backing :4318 , :4317 , :13133 health none none PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) openobserve operator profile :5080 127.0.0.1:5080 none TICK-634 prometheus operator profile :9091 127.0.0.1:9091 none TICK-330 nava-core platform :8080 public, :9090 admin 127.0.0.1:18080 nava_app , nava_audit_reader PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) nava-worker platform :9090 admin none nava_worker PLAN-001 T31b (TICK-159) nava-relay platform :9090 admin none nava_relay PLAN-001 T30b (TICK-85) nava-purge platform :9090 admin none nava_purge PLAN-001 T13 (TICK-143) nava-onboard platform :8081 operator API, :9090 admin none nava_app , nava_onboard PLAN-001 T21 (TICK-234) nava-groupsync platform :9090 admin none nava_groupsync PLAN-001 T25 (TICK-234) \"Introduced by\" names the work that first added the service to the compose file. The story manifest does not list these yet, so they are named in prose here, not linked. temporal-schema (one-shot) Runs setup-schema.sh from the nava/temporal-server image once per up . It connects straight to PostgreSQL as temporal (not through PgBouncer) and creates or upgrades the schema in the temporal and temporal_visibility databases, then exits. It is on temporal-backplane only; temporal-server waits for it to complete. Binaries that are not services Binary What it is Covered on cmd/nava-db the db-roles and migrate one-shots nava-db cmd/nava offline operator CLI, nava audit verify \u003cfile\u003e ; no database, no network the audit-chain page in Security and the Operations section cmd/openapi-compat CI tooling: fails when api/openapi.yaml removes or narrows anything not a runtime component cmd/demo-outbox , cmd/demo-interpreter demos run by scripts under scripts/dev/ excluded: not part of the running platform"},{"url":"system/keycloak.html","title":"keycloak","section":"system","text":"The identity provider. Realm nava is imported at first boot from deploy/compose/keycloak/import . Image nava/keycloak (built from deploy/compose/keycloak/Dockerfile , start --optimized ) Networks default , internal Listeners :8080 HTTP (public and back channel), :9000 management (health; unpublished) Host port 127.0.0.1:18081 ( KEYCLOAK_PORT ) DB role keycloak , owner of database keycloak , connecting directly to postgres:5432 Introduced by PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) What talks to it Browsers and API clients log in on the published port. nava-core : discovery and JWKS on keycloak:8080 , and its health check on :9000 and the token endpoint. nava-onboard : creates the tenant organisation and its admin group (Admin API). nava-groupsync : reads users, groups and memberships (Admin API). Boundary it enforces I2, the tenant comes only from a platform-verified source. The issuer is pinned ( KC_HOSTNAME ), so a token's iss never follows the request's Host header, and every validator trusts exactly that one value. TestComposeKeycloakIssuerPinned and TestEveryTokenValidatorTrustsPinnedIssuer prove it. Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/nava-core.html","title":"nava-core","section":"system","text":"The core API service: every tenant-facing /api/v1 route. It is the only platform service with a published port. Networks default , internal Listeners :8080 public ( NAVA_HTTP_ADDR ), :9090 admin with /metrics , /healthz , /readyz ( NAVA_ADMIN_ADDR ) Host port 127.0.0.1:18080 ( NAVA_CORE_PORT ); the admin port is never published (tunnel 19090) DB roles nava_app on pool nava ; nava_app on pool nava_alloc (record-number allocator); nava_audit_reader on nava (the only service holding it). All through PgBouncer Talks to pgbouncer; keycloak (discovery, JWKS, health); temporal (readiness probe with the nava-core certificate); otel-collector Introduced by PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) /readyz follows PostgreSQL. It never runs migrations and answers the operator onboarding routes 501 : those live in nava-onboard . Boundary it enforces I2. The tenant comes from the validated token through tenant_identity_binding , never from the client. TestGetMeIgnoresSuppliedTenant proves a supplied tenant is ignored. Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/nava-db.html","title":"nava-db (db-roles, migrate: one-shot)","section":"system","text":"One binary, cmd/nava-db , run as two one-shot compose services from the nava/nava-db image. Both exit when done; nava-core never migrates. db-roles migrate Command nava-db roles nava-db migrate Network none ( network_mode: none ) internal Connects over the Unix socket in the pgsocket volume postgres:5432 directly (PgBouncer refuses nava_migrator ) DB role the bootstrap superuser (the only service holding its password, as a file under /run ) nava_migrator , owner of database nava Runs after postgres is healthy db-roles completed Listeners none none db-roles converges every role in db/grants.yaml (no SUPERUSER , CREATEDB , CREATEROLE or BYPASSRLS , no memberships), the databases and their owners, and PgBouncer's lookup function. migrate applies the migrations under goose's session lock, then the generated audit-log policies and grants ( db/audited-tables.yaml ), then the grant manifest, then the catalogue gate. Introduced by PLAN-001 T03. Boundary it enforces I3, least-privilege roles, from one manifest. Every grant comes from db/grants.yaml . TestD28NoSuperuserOrBypassRLS and TestDBRolesRolesHaveNoDangerousAttributes prove no runtime role gains a dangerous attribute; TestMigratorOwnsDatabaseAndIsGrantedToNoOne proves the owner role is never shared. Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/nava-groupsync.html","title":"nava-groupsync","section":"system","text":"Mirrors each tenant's users, groups, memberships and org units under /t/\u003ctenant_id\u003e/ from Keycloak into the identity tables, every NAVA_GROUPSYNC_INTERVAL (default 5m, at most 5m). A principal resolves ( GET /api/v1/me ) only after a cycle has synced it. --once runs one cycle and exits. Network internal Listeners :9090 admin only Host port none (tunnel 19092) DB role nava_groupsync through PgBouncer Talks to keycloak (Admin API read, client nava-groupsync ); pgbouncer; otel-collector Introduced by PLAN-001 T25 (TICK-234) Boundary it enforces I3. nava_groupsync may write only the identity tables, and cannot re-key a group: TestE5GroupsyncCannotRekeyGroups . Its Keycloak account holds only the roles the sync needs: TestGroupsyncAccountFloor . Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/nava-onboard.html","title":"nava-onboard","section":"system","text":"The operator onboarding API, POST /api/v1/operator/tenants , in its own process. It is the only process that holds the onboarding service account's secret and the nava_onboard database URL. Network internal Listeners :8081 operator API ( NAVA_ONBOARD_ADDR ), :9090 admin Host port none: docker compose exec , or tunnel 18082 (API) and 19091 (admin) DB roles nava_app (token checks, registry reads) and nava_onboard (onboarding writes), both through PgBouncer Talks to keycloak (organisation and admin group, Admin API); pgbouncer; otel-collector Introduced by PLAN-001 T21 (TICK-234) Being unpublished means the tenant ingress (nava-core's port) cannot reach it. Boundary it enforces I2. Only allow-listed operators from the pinned issuer may onboard, and a tenant id in the request is ignored: TestOnboardIgnoresSuppliedTenantID , TestForgedIssuerTokenRejectedByOnboard , and TestOnboardDeploymentSeparation for the separate process. Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/nava-purge.html","title":"nava-purge","section":"system","text":"The retention purge: on a ticker, deletes expired idempotency_keys , outbox and workflow_ref rows, and counts dead audit seal lineages. It does not use Temporal and holds no certificate. Network internal Listeners :9090 admin only Host port none (tunnel 19095) DB role nava_purge through PgBouncer Talks to pgbouncer; otel-collector Introduced by PLAN-001 T13 (TICK-143) Boundary it enforces I3. nava_purge may delete only expired rows and read only the columns that decide expiry: TestT13PurgeRoleCannotWidenItsReach . Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/nava-relay.html","title":"nava-relay","section":"system","text":"The outbox relay: claims pending outbox rows of every tenant and starts or signals their workflows. Network internal Listeners :9090 admin only Host port none (tunnel 19093) DB role nava_relay through PgBouncer; dispatch columns of outbox only Talks to temporal (start, signal, with the nava-relay certificate); pgbouncer; otel-collector Introduced by PLAN-001 T30b (TICK-85) /readyz follows both PostgreSQL and Temporal. Boundary it enforces I6. Nothing starts or signals a workflow except the relay, from an outbox row. TestCI6_RelayCoreWorkerOutsideAllowListDenied proves each certificate is held to its own calls. Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/nava-worker.html","title":"nava-worker","section":"system","text":"The Temporal worker: namespace nava , task queue nava-tq . Its client and worker come only from internal/workerguard , and activity code gets no Temporal client. Network internal Listeners :9090 admin only; no public listener Host port none (tunnel 19094) DB role nava_worker through PgBouncer Talks to temporal (poll nava-tq , with the nava-worker certificate); pgbouncer; otel-collector Introduced by PLAN-001 T31b (TICK-159) /readyz follows both PostgreSQL and Temporal. Boundary it enforces I6. A workflow cannot reach another tenant's run: TestCI6_WorkerCrossTenantSignalExternalDeniedWithAlert . Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/openobserve.html","title":"openobserve","section":"system","text":"Local trace and log search: shows what the Collector forwards. Local development only, in the operator profile. Image public.ecr.aws/zinclabs/openobserve:v1.1.2-debug (pin in docs/pins.md ) Networks internal (so the Collector reaches it), operator Listener :5080 (UI and OTLP/HTTP ingest) Host port 127.0.0.1:5080 ( OPENOBSERVE_PORT ) Data volume openobserve-data DB role none Introduced by TICK-634 (replaced the earlier trace UI) Boundary it enforces No ADR-001 section 1 invariant. Its boundary is the publishing rule: only allow-listed UIs publish, and only on loopback. TestComposePublishesOnlyAllowedUIs proves it. Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/otel-collector.html","title":"otel-collector","section":"system","text":"The OpenTelemetry Collector. Every nava-* service sends traces and logs to it ( OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4318 ). Image nava/otel-collector (built from deploy/compose/otel-collector ) Network internal Listeners :4318 OTLP/HTTP, :4317 OTLP/gRPC, :13133 health Host port none Exports to the debug exporter, and openobserve:5080 (OTLP/HTTP, only reachable with the operator profile) DB role none Introduced by PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) Traces are tail-sampled ( OTEL_TAIL_SAMPLE_PERCENT , default 10%); errors and slow requests are always kept. Boundary it enforces No ADR-001 section 1 invariant is owned here. The boundary is the second layer of the telemetry filter: the Collector deletes denied attributes (URL paths and queries, client addresses, user agents, headers) from spans and logs, behind the in-process allow-list in internal/platform/telemetry . TestCollectorDenyListMatchesFilter and TestLogDenyListMatchesCollector keep the two lists equal. Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/pgbouncer.html","title":"pgbouncer","section":"system","text":"The connection pooler between every nava-* service and PostgreSQL, in transaction pooling mode. Image edoburu/pgbouncer:v1.24.1-p1 Network internal Listener :5432 Host port none Pools nava and nava_alloc (the record-number allocator, pool_size=4 ), both on database nava DB role pgbouncer_auth , which may only call pgbouncer.get_auth(text) (the auth_query ) Introduced by PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) What talks to it nava-core , nava-onboard , nava-groupsync , nava-relay , nava-worker and nava-purge . It talks to postgres:5432 . It starts only after migrate completes. Boundary it enforces I3, least-privilege roles. PgBouncer logs in only roles marked pgbouncer_login: true in db/grants.yaml : get_auth returns nothing for a superuser, a BYPASSRLS role, a replication role or nava_migrator . TestGetAuthReachableOnlyByPgBouncerAuth proves only pgbouncer_auth can call the lookup, and TestTenantNotVisibleThroughPgBouncer proves a pooled server connection never carries one transaction's tenant into the next. Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/postgres.html","title":"postgres","section":"system","text":"PostgreSQL 16, the one database server of the stack. It holds the platform database nava and the service databases keycloak , temporal and temporal_visibility . Image postgres:16.10 Networks internal , temporal-backplane Listeners :5432 (TCP), Unix socket in the pgsocket volume Host port none Roles that log in through PgBouncer: nava_app , nava_audit_reader , nava_worker , nava_relay , nava_purge , nava_onboard , nava_groupsync . Direct: nava_migrator (migrate), keycloak , temporal , and the bootstrap superuser over the socket only (db-roles). nava_erasure and nava_backup exist, but no service in the stack runs as them Introduced by PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) What talks to it pgbouncer : the server pools for every nava-* service. migrate , keycloak , temporal-schema , temporal-server : direct TCP connections. db-roles : the superuser, over the Unix socket. db/pg_hba.conf replaces the image's file and opens no other path to the superuser. Local settings: fsync , synchronous_commit and full_page_writes are off (dev data is disposable; reset with docker compose down -v ). max_connections=250 ; every pool must fit under it, and the sum is kept in docs/budgets.md under \"Connection sizing\". Boundary it enforces I1, the tenant wall in the database. Every tenant table has row-level security forced on, keyed on app.tenant_id . TestCI2EveryRoleEveryTenantTable runs every runtime role against every tenant table and proves it sees only its own tenant's rows. TestPgHBA pins the socket-only superuser rule. Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/prometheus.html","title":"prometheus","section":"system","text":"Local metrics and alert rules. Local development only, in the operator profile; no Alertmanager, so a firing alert shows on its Alerts page. Image prom/prometheus:v3.7.3 Networks internal , operator Listener :9091 Host port 127.0.0.1:9091 ( PROMETHEUS_PORT ) Scrapes /metrics on :9090 of nava-core, nava-worker, nava-relay, nava-groupsync, nava-purge and nava-onboard, every 15 s; one-shot jobs are not scraped DB role none Introduced by TICK-330 Boundary it enforces No ADR-001 section 1 invariant. It reads the admin listeners over internal , so they never need publishing: TestComposePublishesNoAdminPort . Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/temporal-server.html","title":"temporal-server","section":"system","text":"The Temporal server, built as nava/temporal-server from temporal-server/ . It runs with --bootstrap : it registers namespace nava and the NavaTenantId search attribute itself, with the temporal-system certificate that only it holds. Network temporal-backplane only Listeners :7233 frontend (client certificates required); internode and membership ports, reachable only on the backplane Host port none DB role temporal , owner of temporal and temporal_visibility , connecting directly Runs after temporal-schema completed Introduced by PLAN-001 T28 Healthy means bootstrapped: the health check dials the frontend and requires the namespace and the search attribute. Clients never reach it directly; they go through the temporal gateway . Boundary it enforces I6, the durable path is entered only through the outbox. Only the gateway's port 7233 is reachable from internal , and the internode port has its own CA. TestCI6_PlatformNetworkCannotReachInternalPorts and TestInternodeRejectsFrontendClientCertificates prove both. Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/temporal-ui.html","title":"temporal-ui","section":"system","text":"The Temporal web UI, for operators. Off by default: it starts only with docker compose --profile operator up -d . Image temporalio/ui:2.40.1 Network operator only Listener :8080 Host port none: make operator-tunnel , or tunnels.sh up (127.0.0.1:18233) Talks to temporal:7233 with the nava-operator certificate, namespace nava DB role none Introduced by PLAN-001 T28 It publishes no port on purpose: a published port is reachable from platform containers through the host. Boundary it enforces I6. The operator certificate may make only a pinned list of calls, so the UI cannot start or signal a workflow: TestCI6_OperatorLimitedToPinnedList . Back to the system map"},{"url":"system/temporal.html","title":"temporal (gateway)","section":"system","text":"A TCP proxy ( temporal-server proxy -listen :7233 -to temporal-server:7233 ) from the same image. It forwards port 7233 and nothing else, holds no certificate, and does not end TLS: each client's mutual TLS reaches the server end to end. Networks internal , operator , temporal-backplane Listener :7233 Host port none DB role none Clients nava-relay (start, signal), nava-worker (poll nava-tq ), nava-core (readiness probe only), temporal-ui Introduced by PLAN-001 T02 (TICK-51) as the Temporal service; a gateway since PLAN-001 T28 Boundary it enforces I6. Because the gateway forwards only 7233, a platform process can speak only the frontend API, and only with its own certificate. The server's authorizer then allows each certificate only its listed calls: TestCI6_UnlistedCertificateDeniedByAuthorizer and TestCI6_RelayCoreWorkerOutsideAllowListDenied . Back to the system map"},{"url":"vision/bounded-cascades.html","title":"I9: Cascades are bounded","section":"vision","text":"I9 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Every write and every outbox row carries a causation record: the id of the origin event and a depth. The pipeline rejects a write beyond a hard, platform-wide depth limit, and rejects a cycle (the same origin seen again on the same record), with a typed error and an audit entry. The limit is platform configuration, never tenant configuration. Where it is enforced The write pipeline ( internal/records/pipeline ) and the outbox ( internal/outbox ), which records causation_id and causation_depth . Proof Annex B CI-10 (a cascade beyond the limit, or a cycle, is rejected with a typed error and audited). The cycle-suppression rule for workflow starts is amendment ADR-001-A5. Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestCI10_DepthBeyondLimitRejectedAndAuditedOnTheRunner , TestCI10_DepthBeyondLimitWritesNothing , TestCI10_SiblingAndSelfRetriggeringWritesAreNotCycles , TestCreateRecordIgnoresCausationFromTheRequest . Read next Enforcement detail: Workflows and Database . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/contracts-configuration-advisory-ai.html","title":"I8: Contracts are the boundary; configuration, not code; AI is advisory","section":"vision","text":"I8 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Tenant code, plugins and AI reach data only through the capability contract or the public API, with a scoped token. They never reach the database or Temporal directly. A tenant change never needs a schema change, with one exception: an operator-approved, measured and audited indexed attribute, run by the migrator outside the tenant's change set. Per-tenant numbering uses a counter table, not sequences. AI may change data only above an explicit confidence threshold, and the decision is recorded. Where it is enforced Ports and adapters in the Go packages (the identity port is the worked example, internal/identity ), a depguard import rule in .golangci.yml , and the numbering allocator. Proof Annex B CI-11 is a static import and lint check, not a Go test: only the identity adapter package may import a Keycloak client. The contract suite TestContract runs the adapter against the port's contract. No test on main covers the AI confidence threshold: no AI code that writes data is on main , so that clause has nothing to prove yet. Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestContract , TestCompileNumberingMode , TestAllocationTransactionIsTenantScoped . Read next Enforcement detail: Security . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/durable-path-outbox.html","title":"I6: The durable path is deterministic, versioned and entered only through the outbox","section":"vision","text":"I6 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Workflow code is deterministic: all I/O, clocks, randomness and tenant code run in activities. A running execution keeps the version it started on. Nothing starts or signals a workflow except the relay, dispatching an outbox row that was committed in the same transaction as the change that caused it. Where it is enforced The transactional outbox ( internal/outbox ), the relay ( internal/relay ), the workflow interpreter ( internal/workflow ) and the Temporal Authorizer, which refuses every other caller ( internal/temporalstack ). Proof Annex B CI-6 (relay start and signal behaviour, certificate and command checks) and CI-7 (payloads carry references only). Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestDemoOutboxTwiceWithOneKeyIsOneWorkflowWithTwoSignalsInOrder , TestCI6_RelayRedeliveredStartAfterCompletionStartsNoSecondRun , TestCI7_ProducerSchemaRejectsRecordDataFields . Read next Enforcement detail: Workflows . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/goals-and-non-goals.html","title":"Goals and non-goals","section":"vision","text":"This page follows ADR-001 and the README, and the Milestone 1 scope as recorded in the ADR-001 reading guide. It adds no goals of its own. Goals Keep tenants apart. One tenant can never read or change another's data. The nine invariants are the rules that guarantee it. Know the tenant from a verified source only (decision D26): the identity provider's token, resolved through a platform lookup table. One predictable API (decision D6): REST at /api/v1 , generated from an OpenAPI contract, with stable error codes, idempotency keys and optimistic concurrency. Reliable background work (decision D22): saved changes start workflows through a transactional outbox and a relay, with workflows kept inside their tenant. Narrow database accounts (decision D28): one account per system actor, none able to skip the tenant wall. Third-party systems behind ports and adapters , so a vendor can be replaced without touching the core (README, design principles). Non-goals for Milestone 1 ADR-001 and its reading guide list what is deliberately left out, because each item adds a path that is not yet secured: Temporal Schedules, cron and reset. Partitioned tables. Tenant self-service administration of the identity provider (residual R3); the platform API does it. GraphQL. It arrives later, as a read surface over the same authorization layer. Encrypting workflow histories per tenant (residual R1). Workflow payloads carry references only. Planned, not on main Stories S12, S18, S19 and S20 are planned and are not described anywhere in this handbook yet; the stories section lists them."},{"url":"vision/idempotent-side-effects.html","title":"I7: Every side effect is idempotent","section":"vision","text":"I7 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says A retry, a replay or a redelivery never acts twice. A repeated request with the same idempotency key returns the same response; the same key with a different body is a conflict. A redelivered workflow start or signal is applied once. Where it is enforced The idempotency middleware and the pipeline ( internal/platform/idempotency , internal/records/pipeline ), the outbox dedupe key ( internal/outbox ) and the relay's reject-duplicate start ( internal/relay ). Proof Annex B CI-8 (idempotency replay and mismatch) and CI-6 (redelivered start and signal). Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestIdempotencyKeyReplays , TestIdempotencyKeyMismatchConflicts , TestCI10_DuplicateDedupeKeyWritesOneRow , TestCI6_RelayRedeliveredSignalAppliedOnceAcrossContinueAsNew . Read next Enforcement detail: Workflows and Security . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/index.html","title":"Vision","section":"vision","text":"What the product is The platform ( nava-core ) is a multi-tenant ticketing platform. Many customer organisations, called tenants, share one installation, one database and one set of services. The main risk of that design is one tenant seeing or changing another tenant's data, so the architecture is built around keeping tenants apart. ADR-001 records the ground rules; see goals and non-goals . Who uses it Tenant users and applications call the REST API at /api/v1 with a token issued by the identity provider. Tenant administrators publish entity types, set access rules and manage users and groups through the platform API. Platform operators onboard tenants, run migrations and administer the Temporal workflow engine. Engineers and the product owner build and accept the platform; this handbook is for them. Read next Goals and non-goals The nine invariants , one page each: I1 , I2 , I3 , I4 , I5 , I6 , I7 , I8 , I9 Glossary Known technical risks , kept by the CTO outside the handbook"},{"url":"vision/invariants.html","title":"The nine invariants","section":"vision","text":"An invariant is a rule that must always hold. ADR-001 section 1 lists nine, I1 to I9. Later designs cannot break one without a new ADR signed by the product owner. Each page below says the rule in plain words, names the layer that enforces it and lists the tests that prove it. ID Invariant I1 Tenant wall in the database I2 The tenant comes only from a platform-verified source I3 Least-privilege database roles I4 One write pipeline with optimistic concurrency I5 One authorization layer, inside the query I6 The durable path is deterministic, versioned and entered only through the outbox I7 Every side effect is idempotent I8 Contracts are the boundary; configuration, not code; AI is advisory I9 Cascades are bounded Decisions that are not invariants Decision G7 (one shared database, shared tables, forced row-level security, tenant_id leading every tenant-table index) is recorded as a decision, not an invariant. Moving a large tenant to its own database is an operator option. Isolation does not depend on the topology: I1 and I3 keep it. Known technical risks The known technical risks are tracked in the CTO's register, outside the handbook."},{"url":"vision/least-privilege-roles.html","title":"I3: Least-privilege database roles","section":"vision","text":"I3 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says No account the platform runs as is a superuser, can skip row-level security, owns an object, or is a member of any other role. Nothing is granted to PUBLIC , including through default privileges. Every permission comes from one manifest, and CI compares the live database with it. No SECURITY DEFINER function or policy-bypassing view is reachable from a runtime account. Where it is enforced The one-shot migrator and the roles job ( internal/db ), the grants manifest db/grants.yaml , and the catalogue checks run against a live database. Proof Annex B CI-3 (role membership and ownership), CI-4 (privileges equal the manifest exactly) and CI-9 (each system actor can do only its own job). Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestD28NoSuperuserOrBypassRLS , TestManifestDetectsAndReportsGrantDrift , TestDBRolesNewPlatformDatabaseHasNoPublicGrants , TestT10GateEmptySecurityDefinerExceptions . Read next Enforcement detail: Database and Security . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/one-authorization-layer.html","title":"I5: One authorization layer, inside the query","section":"vision","text":"I5 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Access rules are compiled into SQL, and the single-record check in Go gives the same answer as the SQL. Field masks apply on write and on read. A record the caller cannot read, a record of another tenant and a record that does not exist all produce the same response. Where it is enforced The ACL compiler and checker ( internal/authz ) used by the records pipeline and the list queries ( internal/records/pipeline , internal/server ). Proof Annex B CI-8 (byte-identical 404s, a masked-field filter returns 400) and the I5 agreement suite in internal/tenancy/isolation . Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestI5Agreement , TestCompileReadAgreesWithAllows , TestRecordFourNotFoundCasesAreByteIdentical , TestMaskedFieldInFilterIsRefusedBeforeSQL . Read next Enforcement detail: Security . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/one-write-pipeline.html","title":"I4: One write pipeline with optimistic concurrency","section":"vision","text":"I4 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Every writer goes through the same steps: authorisation, defaults and computed values, JSON Schema validation, declarative checks, then commit with the audit entry in one transaction. Every update carries the version of the record it expects. A write against a stale version is a typed conflict, never last-writer-wins. Where it is enforced The records pipeline ( internal/records/pipeline ), reached from the API handlers in internal/server . An architecture test keeps record writers inside the pipeline. Proof Annex B CI-8 (a stale If-Match returns 412) and the architecture tests in internal/records/arch_test.go . Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestEveryWriteBumpsVersionOnce , TestIfMatchVersions , TestRecordWritersOnlyInPipeline , TestPipelineImportRule . Read next Enforcement detail: Security and Database . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/tenant-source.html","title":"I2: The tenant comes only from a platform-verified source","section":"vision","text":"I2 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says The tenant of a request is never taken from anything the caller supplies. It comes from a validated token, resolved through the platform's identity binding table (decision D26), or from a row the platform itself wrote under row-level security (the outbox row, decision D22). Request bodies, payloads, script arguments, workflow inputs, signal bodies and search attributes are never a source. Temporal is a path like any other. Where it is enforced The identity port and its Keycloak adapter ( internal/identity , internal/identity/keycloak ) resolve the token to a tenant; the API layer passes only that value on; the relay reads the tenant from the outbox row; the Temporal Authorizer checks workflow ids and commands ( internal/temporalstack ). Proof Annex B CI-5 (a catalogue of rejected tokens: zero or two organizations, unknown issuer, wrong-realm key, ID token as access token, foreign IdP, duplicate binding) and CI-6 (Temporal). Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestCI5 , TestClaimsForIgnoresClientTenant , TestGetMeIgnoresSuppliedTenant , TestCI6_WorkerWrongTenantSignalDroppedWithAlertWorkflowKeepsRunning . Read next Enforcement detail: Security and Workflows . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/tenant-wall.html","title":"I1: Tenant wall in the database","section":"vision","text":"I1 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Every table that holds tenant data carries a tenant_id , has row-level security enabled and forced , and has a policy that compares tenant_id with the tenant the session declared. If a session declares no tenant, a query returns no rows or an error, never another tenant's rows. Unique keys and foreign keys include tenant_id . A policy that does not test the tenant must be on a short, named allow-list. Where it is enforced The database itself: PostgreSQL row-level security on every tenant table ( db/migrations ), plus the per-request tenant setting made by internal/tenancy and internal/db . Proof ADR-001 Annex B CI-1 (catalogue checks) and CI-2 (no tenant set gives zero rows or an error, for every runtime role and every tenant table). Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestCI2EveryRoleEveryTenantTable , TestT11TenantTablesFailClosed , TestT09PolicyAllowList . Read next Enforcement detail: Database . All nine invariants: the invariants page . Note The shared database and shared tables are decision G7 , not an invariant: topology is a performance and deployment choice. I1 and I3 are what keep tenants apart, whatever the topology."},{"url":"workflows/index.html","title":"Workflows","section":"workflows","text":"TODO: the durable workflows and their activities."}];
